/* generated by tools/build-content.js from content-source/quiz-json - do not edit by hand */
window.CONTENT = {"ch01":{"title":"Chapter 1 - Basic Principles of Insurance","flashcards":[{"front":"Define insurance.","back":"Transfer of risk through a legal contract, or pooling of funds, spreading one person's risk across a larger group.","topic":"Definition","memory":""},{"front":"Who owns a mutual company and who gets its dividends?","back":"Policyholders own it. Dividends are paid to policyholders from divisible surplus, and they vote for the board.","topic":"Stock vs mutual","memory":"MUTUAL = MEMBERS"},{"front":"Stock company: typical policy type?","back":"Typically nonparticipating, with profits to stockholders. This is the usual pattern, not an absolute rule.","topic":"Stock vs mutual","memory":""},{"front":"Mutualization vs demutualization?","back":"Mutualization: a stock company becomes a mutual. Demutualization: a mutual becomes a stock company.","topic":"Stock vs mutual","memory":""},{"front":"Risk retention group (RRG)?","back":"Group-owned insurer covering liability risks. Licensed in one state, operates in all states.","topic":"Company types","memory":""},{"front":"Ceding company vs reinsurer?","back":"The ceding company passes part of the risk away. The reinsurer accepts it. Net retention is what the ceder keeps.","topic":"Reinsurance","memory":"CEDE = GIVE AWAY"},{"front":"Domestic, foreign, alien insurer?","back":"Domestic: home state. Foreign: another U.S. state. Alien: principal office outside the U.S.","topic":"Classification","memory":"Alien = Alien country"},{"front":"Admitted vs non-admitted insurer?","back":"Admitted holds a Certificate of Authority from the state. Non-admitted does not.","topic":"Classification","memory":""},{"front":"Lloyd's of London is what?","back":"Not an insurer. It is a marketplace where individuals and companies underwrite unusual risks.","topic":"Other entities","memory":""},{"front":"Agent vs broker: whom does each represent?","back":"Agent represents the insurer. Broker represents the client/insured. A public adjuster represents the insured.","topic":"Key people","memory":"AGENT = ATTACHED to insurer; BROKER = BUYER's side"},{"front":"Three types of agent authority?","back":"Express (stated), implied (needed to do the job), apparent (looks real to the public due to principal's conduct).","topic":"Agent authority","memory":"Said / Needed / Looks real"},{"front":"Captive agent vs independent agent?","back":"Captive (career) agent works for one insurer. Independent agent represents multiple insurers.","topic":"Selling","memory":""},{"front":"McCarran-Ferguson Act (1945) main point?","back":"States keep regulating insurance; federal laws apply only to the extent insurance is not regulated by state law.","topic":"Federal timeline","memory":""},{"front":"Four NAIC objectives?","back":"Uniformity in state laws, efficiency in regulation, consumer protection, and preserving state regulation.","topic":"NAIC","memory":""},{"front":"Can a producer cite guaranty association protection in a sale?","back":"No. Producers cannot use guaranty association protection in sales presentations.","topic":"Guaranty associations","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Insurance is best defined as:","choices":["Transfer of risk through a legal contract or pooling of funds","Elimination of all risk","A savings plan managed by the state","A guarantee against all loss"],"answer":0,"explain":"Insurance transfers risk by contract or pooling. It does not eliminate risk.","topic":"Definition"},{"q":"Who owns a mutual insurance company?","choices":["Stockholders","The state","Its policyholders","Its agents"],"answer":2,"explain":"A mutual has no stockholders; policyholders own it. Stockholders own stock companies.","topic":"Stock vs mutual"},{"q":"An insurer whose principal office is in Germany and sells in the U.S. is:","choices":["Domestic","Foreign","Alien","Non-admitted by definition"],"answer":2,"explain":"Alien means principal office outside the U.S. Foreign means another U.S. state.","topic":"Classification"},{"q":"The ceding company in a reinsurance arrangement is the one that:","choices":["Accepts the risk","Passes part of the risk away","Regulates the reinsurer","Sells only one line"],"answer":1,"explain":"To cede is to give away. The reinsurer is the company that accepts the risk.","topic":"Reinsurance"},{"q":"Which type of agent authority is created when the company's conduct makes an agent look authorized to the public?","choices":["Express","Implied","Apparent","Fiduciary"],"answer":2,"explain":"Apparent authority comes from the principal's conduct. Implied is what is needed to do the job but not stated.","topic":"Agent authority"},{"q":"A risk retention group is licensed in how many states and operates in how many?","choices":["One licensed, operates in all","All licensed, operates in one","Two licensed, operates in two","One licensed, operates in one"],"answer":0,"explain":"RRGs and RPGs are licensed in one state and may operate in all states.","topic":"Company types"},{"q":"Which cost index period is typically used for the net payment cost index?","choices":["1 or 2 years","5 years only","10 or 20 years","30 years only"],"answer":2,"explain":"Net payment and surrender cost indexes compare cost over a set period, usually 10 or 20 years.","topic":"Disclosures"},{"q":"Under 18 U.S.C. 1033, a felon barred from the insurance business under 1033(e) is barred for up to:","choices":["1 year","5 years","15 years","Life"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 1033(e) bar is up to 5 years. The 10 and 15 year figures relate to imprisonment, and 15 applies only when an insurer was placed in conservation, rehabilitation or liquidation.","topic":"Federal timeline"},{"q":"Which statement about stock companies is NOT a safe rule?","choices":["Stockholders own them","Policyholders cannot vote for the board","Stock companies can never issue participating policies","They aim to earn profit for shareholders"],"answer":2,"explain":"Nonparticipating is the typical pattern for stock companies, but exceptions exist, so \"never\" is not a safe rule.","topic":"Stock vs mutual"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of Lloyd's of London?","choices":["It is a marketplace for unusual risks","It is itself a single insurer","Individuals and companies underwrite there","It is a typical stock insurance company owned by shareholders"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lloyd's is not an insurer at all; it is a marketplace where members underwrite unusual risks.","topic":"Other entities"},{"q":"Which is NOT a correct statement about the 1999 Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act?","choices":["It requires a privacy notice and opt-out before sharing nonpublic financial information with nonaffiliated third parties","It lets banks, insurers and securities firms combine","It requires affirmative consent before sharing health information with any third party","Health information is governed mainly by HIPAA and state law"],"answer":2,"explain":"GLBA is a notice-and-opt-out law for financial information. It is not the health-information consent rule.","topic":"Federal timeline"},{"q":"Which statement about the broker is correct?","choices":["The broker represents the insurer in disputes","The broker represents the client/insured","The broker is always a captive agent","The broker works only for one insurer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Broker = client's side. In a dispute, the agent represents the insurer.","topic":"Key people"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Carl sells only for one insurance company and is paid by that company. His client Maria also visited another office and received quotes from several insurers.","q":"How should Carl and the quote-shopping office be described?","choices":["Carl is independent; the other office is captive","Carl is a captive agent; the other office is an independent agent or broker","Both are captive agents","Carl is a broker; the other office is a public adjuster"],"answer":1,"explain":"One insurer = captive/career. Quotes from several insurers = independent agent or broker.","topic":"Selling"},{"situation":"Ray's insurer denies his claim. Ray hires someone, paid by Ray, to fight the denial and settle the claim.","q":"Whom does this person work for?","choices":["The insurer","The insured","The state insurance department","The reinsurer"],"answer":1,"explain":"A public adjuster represents the insured, unlike the insurer's own adjuster.","topic":"Key people"},{"situation":"A company says it is authorized to do business in Florida, but its headquarters is in Canada.","q":"How is it classified in Florida?","choices":["Domestic","Foreign","Alien","Mutualized"],"answer":2,"explain":"Principal office outside the U.S. makes it alien. Foreign would mean headquartered in another U.S. state.","topic":"Classification"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Domestic insurer","match":"Principal office in the state where it is authorized"},{"term":"Alien insurer","match":"Principal office outside the United States"},{"term":"Ceding company","match":"Passes part of its risk to a reinsurer"},{"term":"Public adjuster","match":"Represents the insured in settling claims"},{"term":"Apparent authority","match":"Created by principal's conduct; looks real to others"},{"term":"Demutualization","match":"Mutual company becomes a stock company"}]},"ch02":{"title":"Chapter 2 - Nature of Insurance, Risk, Perils, and Hazards","flashcards":[{"front":"Peril vs hazard vs loss?","back":"Peril: the direct cause of loss. Hazard: a condition that increases the chance of a peril. Loss: the decrease in value.","topic":"Peril/hazard/loss","memory":"Peril = the Pow; Hazard = the Hint"},{"front":"Law of large numbers?","back":"As more similar risks are grouped, total losses become more predictable. Insurers predict group losses, not one person's loss.","topic":"Foundations","memory":"BIG GROUP = BIG PREDICTION"},{"front":"Principle of indemnity?","back":"Put the insured back in the same financial position as before the loss, with no profit from the loss.","topic":"Indemnity","memory":""},{"front":"Is life insurance a contract of indemnity?","back":"No. It is a valued contract that pays a fixed amount regardless of actual financial loss.","topic":"Indemnity","memory":""},{"front":"Which health coverage is indemnity vs valued?","back":"Expense-reimbursement medical is indemnity. Fixed-amount products (hospital indemnity, critical illness, individual disability income) act like valued contracts.","topic":"Indemnity","memory":"Pays the bills = indemnity; pays a set amount = valued"},{"front":"Adverse selection?","back":"Higher-risk people are more likely to seek and keep insurance, which works against the insurer. Underwriting is the solution.","topic":"Adverse selection","memory":""},{"front":"Speculative vs pure risk?","back":"Speculative risk can produce loss or gain and is not insurable. Pure risk can only produce loss and is insurable.","topic":"Risk types","memory":"Pure = Possible Only Loss"},{"front":"Seven elements of an insurable risk?","back":"Chance of loss, definite and measurable, predictable, not catastrophic, substantial exposure, random selection, economically feasible.","topic":"Insurable risk","memory":""},{"front":"Standard, substandard, preferred risk?","back":"Standard: average loss potential. Substandard: higher, so higher premium or reduced or denied coverage. Preferred: lower loss potential.","topic":"Risk classes","memory":""},{"front":"Named perils vs open perils?","back":"Named: only listed perils covered. Open: everything covered except specific exclusions.","topic":"Perils","memory":""},{"front":"Direct vs indirect loss?","back":"Direct: caused immediately by the peril. Indirect (consequential): a secondary result of the direct loss.","topic":"Loss","memory":""},{"front":"Accident vs occurrence?","back":"Accident: unexpected and sudden at a specific time and place. Occurrence: any loss-causing event, including long-term exposure. Every accident is an occurrence.","topic":"Loss","memory":""},{"front":"Physical hazard?","back":"A tangible, observable condition such as icy walkways, poor lighting or high blood pressure.","topic":"Hazards","memory":"Physical = you can SEE it"},{"front":"Moral vs morale hazard?","back":"Moral: intentional dishonesty or fraud. Morale: unintentional carelessness arising from having insurance.","topic":"Hazards","memory":"Moral = Malicious; Morale = careless"},{"front":"Four steps of risk management?","back":"Identify risks, choose a method, implement the plan, review and adjust periodically.","topic":"Risk management","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"The direct, specific cause of a loss is called a:","choices":["Hazard","Peril","Risk","Exposure"],"answer":1,"explain":"A peril is the cause (fire, theft). A hazard is a condition that makes a peril more likely.","topic":"Peril/hazard/loss"},{"q":"Which type of risk can result in loss or gain and is not insurable?","choices":["Pure risk","Speculative risk","Standard risk","Substandard risk"],"answer":1,"explain":"Speculative risk, such as gambling or the stock market, includes a chance of gain.","topic":"Risk types"},{"q":"A hazard that is intentional dishonesty is called:","choices":["Physical hazard","Morale hazard","Moral hazard","Peril"],"answer":2,"explain":"Moral hazard is intentional (fraud). Morale hazard is careless, not on purpose.","topic":"Hazards"},{"q":"Under the law of large numbers, an insurer can best predict:","choices":["The loss of one individual","Total losses for a large group","Which person will file a claim","The exact date of each loss"],"answer":1,"explain":"Large groups are predictable. Individuals are not.","topic":"Foundations"},{"q":"What risk-management step comes immediately after choosing a method?","choices":["Identify risks","Implement the plan","Review and adjust","Avoid all risk"],"answer":1,"explain":"The order is identify, choose, implement, review.","topic":"Risk management"},{"q":"Under a named-perils policy, a loss from a peril that is not listed is:","choices":["Covered","Not covered","Covered at half value","Covered only if sudden"],"answer":1,"explain":"Named perils cover only the listed perils. Open perils cover everything not excluded.","topic":"Perils"},{"q":"Which describes a substandard risk?","choices":["Lower loss potential, lower premium","Average loss potential","Higher loss potential, possibly higher premium or denial","No loss potential"],"answer":2,"explain":"Substandard means higher than average loss potential. Preferred is the lower one.","topic":"Risk classes"},{"q":"Which element of an insurable risk means the loss should happen by chance and not be controlled by the insured?","choices":["Chance of loss","Predictable","Not catastrophic","Economic feasibility"],"answer":0,"explain":"The insured should have no control over whether the event occurs.","topic":"Insurable risk"},{"q":"Which is NOT a hazard type?","choices":["Physical","Moral","Morale","Speculative"],"answer":3,"explain":"Speculative describes a kind of risk, not a hazard. The three hazards are physical, moral, morale.","topic":"Hazards"},{"q":"Which statement about indemnity is NOT correct?","choices":["The insured should not profit from a loss","It applies to property and expense-reimbursement health","It applies to life insurance as a valued contract","It aims to restore the pre-loss financial position"],"answer":2,"explain":"Life insurance is a valued contract, not indemnity. It pays a fixed amount.","topic":"Indemnity"},{"q":"Which statement about accidents is NOT correct?","choices":["An accident is unexpected and unintended","An accident is sudden at a specific time and place","An accident must have witnesses","Every accident is an occurrence"],"answer":2,"explain":"Witnesses are not a legal element of an accident. An accident is unexpected and unintended.","topic":"Loss"},{"q":"Which is NOT an example of a physical hazard?","choices":["Icy walkway","Poor lighting","Improperly stored chemicals","Exaggerating a claim"],"answer":3,"explain":"Exaggerating a claim is a moral hazard (intentional dishonesty), not a tangible condition.","topic":"Hazards"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Jim has a history of heart disease and applies for life insurance. He plans to leave out his doctor visits on the application.","q":"Which concern best describes Jim's planned omission?","choices":["Morale hazard","Moral hazard (intentional dishonesty)","Physical hazard","Speculative risk"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hiding facts on purpose is intentional dishonesty, which is moral hazard. It also feeds adverse selection.","topic":"Hazards"},{"situation":"Ann leaves her car unlocked with the keys inside because she figures her full coverage will pay if it is stolen. She does not intend for it to be stolen.","q":"Which hazard is this?","choices":["Moral hazard","Morale hazard","Physical hazard","Pure peril"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unintentional carelessness arising from having insurance is morale hazard. Moral would require intent.","topic":"Hazards"},{"situation":"A business owner installs sprinklers and fire doors to lower how bad a fire loss could be.","q":"Which risk-handling method is this?","choices":["Avoidance","Transfer","Reduction (loss control)","Retention"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reduction lowers the severity of a loss. Avoidance would remove the hazard entirely.","topic":"Risk management"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Peril","match":"Direct cause of a loss, such as fire"},{"term":"Hazard","match":"Condition that increases the chance a peril occurs"},{"term":"Pure risk","match":"Chance of loss only; insurable"},{"term":"Moral hazard","match":"Intentional dishonesty or fraud"},{"term":"Morale hazard","match":"Unintentional carelessness due to having insurance"},{"term":"Open perils","match":"Everything covered except what is excluded"}]},"ch03":{"title":"Chapter 3 - Legal Concepts of Insurance","flashcards":[{"front":"Four essential elements of a valid insurance contract?","back":"Offer and acceptance, consideration, legal purpose, competent parties. All four must be present.","topic":"Contract elements","memory":"OCLC: Only Contracts Legally Count"},{"front":"Application submitted without premium?","back":"It is an invitation to the insurer, not an offer. A counter-offer voids the original offer.","topic":"Contract elements","memory":""},{"front":"Aleatory contract?","back":"Unequal exchange: one party may receive far more than it gave, depending on a future event.","topic":"Contract features","memory":"AUAPC"},{"front":"Contract of adhesion?","back":"Drafted by one party (the insurer), take it or leave it. Ambiguity is read against the insurer.","topic":"Contract features","memory":""},{"front":"Unilateral contract?","back":"Only one party (the insurer) makes a binding promise. The applicant can stop paying premiums.","topic":"Contract features","memory":""},{"front":"Valued vs indemnity contract?","back":"Valued pays a predetermined amount (life, fixed-benefit products). Indemnity pays actual loss up to limits (property, casualty, expense-reimbursement health).","topic":"Valued vs indemnity","memory":""},{"front":"What is insurable interest?","back":"A financial or economic stake in the insured person or property. Without it, the contract is an invalid wager.","topic":"Insurable interest","memory":""},{"front":"When must insurable interest exist for life and health?","back":"At the time of application only.","topic":"Insurable interest","memory":"Life/Health = Application"},{"front":"When must insurable interest exist for property and casualty?","back":"At both application and claim.","topic":"Insurable interest","memory":"Pay Attention to Both"},{"front":"Warranty vs representation vs concealment?","back":"Warranty: guaranteed true. Representation: believed true. Concealment: failure to reveal a known material fact.","topic":"Good faith","memory":"I guarantee / I believe / I didn't say"},{"front":"Waiver vs estoppel?","back":"Waiver: voluntarily giving up a known right. Estoppel: a party is stopped from denying what it said or did when another relied on it and was harmed.","topic":"Waiver/estoppel","memory":""},{"front":"Three elements of estoppel?","back":"False representation, reliance by the other party, and harm (financial loss).","topic":"Waiver/estoppel","memory":""},{"front":"Agent vs broker: whom does each represent?","back":"Agent represents the insurer; broker represents the insured/buyer. In a dispute, the agent represents the insurer.","topic":"Agency","memory":""},{"front":"Subrogation?","back":"The insurer's right to recover from the third party who caused the loss after paying its insured.","topic":"Legal doctrines","memory":"SUBSTITUTE: steps into insured's shoes"},{"front":"Parol evidence rule?","back":"The written contract prevails over oral statements made before it. Prior verbal statements cannot change written terms.","topic":"Legal doctrines","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which is NOT one of the four essential elements of a valid insurance contract?","choices":["Consideration","Legal purpose","Competent parties","Witnessed signature"],"answer":3,"explain":"The four are offer and acceptance, consideration, legal purpose, competent parties. A witness is not among them.","topic":"Contract elements"},{"q":"A contract that is drafted by the insurer and read against the insurer when ambiguous is:","choices":["Aleatory","Adhesion","Unilateral","Conditional"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adhesion means take it or leave it, so ambiguity favors the insured.","topic":"Contract features"},{"q":"Insurable interest is best described as:","choices":["Interest earned on cash value","A financial stake in the insured person or property","The insurer's profit","The producer's commission"],"answer":1,"explain":"Insurable interest is the buyer's financial stake. Without it the contract is a wager.","topic":"Insurable interest"},{"q":"A statement believed by the applicant to be true is a:","choices":["Warranty","Representation","Concealment","Counter-offer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Representation is believed true. A warranty is guaranteed true in all respects.","topic":"Good faith"},{"q":"Bob and Sherry bought life policies on each other while married, then divorced and kept the policies. When must insurable interest have existed?","choices":["At application only","At claim only","At both application and claim","At every premium due date"],"answer":0,"explain":"For life insurance, interest is required at application. The divorce does not void the policy.","topic":"Insurable interest"},{"q":"Which element must exist for property insurance but is NOT required at claim time for life insurance?","choices":["Offer and acceptance","Insurable interest at the time of claim","Consideration","Legal purpose"],"answer":1,"explain":"Property and casualty need interest at application and claim. Life only needs it at application.","topic":"Insurable interest"},{"q":"The three elements of estoppel are false representation, reliance, and:","choices":["Harm","Rescission","Subrogation","Consent"],"answer":0,"explain":"Estoppel requires a false representation, reliance on it, and resulting harm.","topic":"Waiver/estoppel"},{"q":"Which can bind coverage?","choices":["Solicitor","Agent","Policyowner","Beneficiary"],"answer":1,"explain":"An agent can bind the insurer. A solicitor can only find and obtain applicants.","topic":"Agency"},{"q":"Which is NOT true about void contracts?","choices":["They lack legal effect","They are missing an essential element","They are enforceable until one party sets them aside","A contract for an illegal purpose is an example"],"answer":2,"explain":"A void contract is not enforceable. A voidable one is binding until voided.","topic":"Void vs voidable"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT a typical E&O exclusion?","choices":["Fraudulent acts","Criminal behavior","Failing to make a requested policy change","Intentional harm"],"answer":2,"explain":"Failing to make a requested policy change is a typical E&O covered loss. Fraud, crime and intentional harm are excluded.","topic":"E&O"},{"q":"Which statement about brokers is NOT correct?","choices":["A broker represents the buyer in a sale","A broker may place coverage with multiple insurers","A broker represents the insurer in disputes","An agent is the insurer's representative"],"answer":2,"explain":"A broker represents the buyer, not the insurer. In disputes, the agent represents the insurer.","topic":"Agency"},{"q":"Which is NOT a person generally lacking capacity to contract?","choices":["A minor","A mentally incompetent person","A person under coercion","A beneficiary who is not the applicant"],"answer":3,"explain":"Beneficiaries and non-applicant insureds are not parties, so they do not need legal capacity.","topic":"Contract elements"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"An agent tells an applicant that the policy covers all damages. The applicant relies on this and buys no more coverage. A claim is later denied because the policy does not cover that loss.","q":"Which doctrine may prevent the insurer from denying the claim?","choices":["Subrogation","Parol evidence","Estoppel","Rescission"],"answer":2,"explain":"False representation, reliance and harm are the three elements of estoppel.","topic":"Waiver/estoppel"},{"situation":"An insurer pays for repairs to a policyholder's car after a crash caused by another driver. The insurer then wants to recover its payment.","q":"What right does the insurer use?","choices":["Subrogation","Waiver","Reasonable expectations","Coinsurance"],"answer":0,"explain":"Subrogation lets the insurer step into the insured's shoes and pursue the at-fault driver.","topic":"Legal doctrines"},{"situation":"Sarah owns a car with an auto policy. She tries to buy a policy on her neighbor Mike's car, in which she has no financial stake.","q":"What is the problem?","choices":["Missing insurable interest, so it is a wager","The contract is conditional","The policy is unilateral","She is a solicitor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Without insurable interest in the property, the contract cannot be valid.","topic":"Insurable interest"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Warranty","match":"Statement guaranteed true in all respects"},{"term":"Representation","match":"Statement believed true to the best of knowledge"},{"term":"Waiver","match":"Voluntary giving up of a known right"},{"term":"Subrogation","match":"Insurer's right to recover from the at-fault third party"},{"term":"Aleatory","match":"Unequal exchange depending on a future event"},{"term":"Rescission","match":"Making the contract null and void as if it never existed"}]},"ch04":{"title":"Chapter 4 - Life Underwriting","flashcards":[{"front":"What is underwriting?","back":"Research and assessment of possible policyholders to decide eligibility and rate. Also called risk selection; it fights adverse selection.","topic":"Underwriting purpose","memory":""},{"front":"Who decides on coverage: agent or home office?","back":"The home office underwriter decides, classifies the risk and sets the rate. The agent collects and submits and cannot issue policies.","topic":"Roles","memory":"Agent collects, home office decides"},{"front":"When must insurable interest exist for life insurance?","back":"Only at policy inception (application), not when proceeds are paid.","topic":"Insurable interest","memory":""},{"front":"Creditor's insurable interest in a debtor?","back":"Yes, but only up to the debt plus accrued interest.","topic":"Insurable interest","memory":"The debt + interest, nothing more"},{"front":"Three parts of the application?","back":"Part I: general applicant information. Part II: medical and health history. Part III: agent's report.","topic":"Application","memory":""},{"front":"Are application statements warranties or representations?","back":"Representations: substantially true to the best of the applicant's knowledge. An insurer must prove materiality to void for a misrepresentation.","topic":"Application","memory":""},{"front":"Conditional receipt?","back":"Most common receipt. Coverage is effective only if the applicant proves insurable, back to the application or exam date. Otherwise the premium is refunded.","topic":"Premium receipts","memory":"Covered IF you're insurable"},{"front":"Binding receipt?","back":"Unconditional. Guarantees coverage until the insurer formally rejects the application.","topic":"Premium receipts","memory":"Guaranteed until we say NO"},{"front":"Trial application?","back":"An application submitted without the initial premium. It is not effective until a premium is collected.","topic":"Premium receipts","memory":""},{"front":"What is the MIB?","back":"Medical Information Bureau, the shared database of health information from past applications, provided to member insurers.","topic":"Information sources","memory":""},{"front":"Attending Physician's Statement (APS)?","back":"A physician's report on specific conditions, requested when there is a question. The applicant must sign a release.","topic":"Information sources","memory":""},{"front":"Four risk classes?","back":"Preferred (reduced premium), standard (regular), substandard (higher premium or rejected), declined (application rejected).","topic":"Risk classes","memory":""},{"front":"Backdating?","back":"Setting an earlier effective date than the application date to use a lower age. The maximum is typically 6 months, as limited by state regulation. Prohibited for variable contracts.","topic":"Policy issue","memory":""},{"front":"Free-look period?","back":"Time to return an individual life policy for a full refund. In Louisiana it is 10 days from receipt of the policy.","topic":"Free look","memory":""},{"front":"What must a producer do for a replacement?","back":"Give a Notice Regarding Replacement, inform the existing insurer, and use the state-mandated replacement disclosure form.","topic":"Replacement","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Underwriting is also known as:","choices":["Claim adjustment","Risk selection","Reinsurance","Surrender processing"],"answer":1,"explain":"Underwriting evaluates and selects risks. Adjusting relates to claims.","topic":"Underwriting purpose"},{"q":"Which part of the life application contains the agent's report?","choices":["Part I","Part II","Part III","The authorization form"],"answer":2,"explain":"Part I is general information, Part II is medical, Part III is the agent's report.","topic":"Application"},{"q":"The risk class that pays a reduced premium is:","choices":["Standard","Preferred","Substandard","Declined"],"answer":1,"explain":"Preferred is the lowest-risk class. Standard pays regular premium.","topic":"Risk classes"},{"q":"Which database holds health information from past applications?","choices":["MIB","APS","ACA","NAIC"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Medical Information Bureau shares health history among member insurers.","topic":"Information sources"},{"q":"A creditor's insurable interest in a debtor is limited to:","choices":["Twice the debt","The debt plus accrued interest","Any amount the creditor wants","Nothing; creditors have no interest"],"answer":1,"explain":"Creditor interest is capped at the debt plus interest.","topic":"Insurable interest"},{"q":"Which receipt guarantees coverage until the insurer formally rejects the application?","choices":["Conditional receipt","Binding receipt","Inspection receipt","Approval receipt"],"answer":1,"explain":"Binding is unconditional until rejection. Conditional depends on the applicant being insurable.","topic":"Premium receipts"},{"q":"Which group lists the three credit bureaus used in underwriting?","choices":["Experian, Equifax, TransUnion","MIB, APS, NAIC","A.M. Best, Fitch, Moody's","FTC, SEC, FDIC"],"answer":0,"explain":"The three credit bureaus are Experian, Equifax and TransUnion. A.M. Best and others are rating services.","topic":"Information sources"},{"q":"How many signatures are involved when the owner is a third party?","choices":["One","Two","Three: applicant, proposed insured, producer","Four"],"answer":2,"explain":"Third-party ownership needs the applicant, the proposed insured and the producer.","topic":"Application"},{"q":"Which statement about the agent in underwriting is NOT true?","choices":["Collects the initial premium and issues a receipt","Completes the application accurately","Issues the policy","Submits the file to the home office"],"answer":2,"explain":"Agents cannot issue policies. The insurer issues them after underwriting.","topic":"Roles"},{"q":"Which is NOT true about an incomplete application?","choices":["It is returned to the agent","It can delay commissions","It can delay issue and delivery","The underwriter should fill in the blanks"],"answer":3,"explain":"Incomplete applications are returned to the agent. The underwriter does not complete them.","topic":"Application"},{"q":"Which is NOT a way an insurer may handle a substandard risk?","choices":["Rated policy (extra charge)","Exclusion rider","Reject the application","Reduce the premium below standard"],"answer":3,"explain":"A substandard risk gets a higher premium, an exclusion, or rejection. Lower premium is for preferred.","topic":"Risk classes"},{"q":"Which is NOT a requirement for a replacement?","choices":["Notice Regarding Replacement","Inform the existing insurer","State-mandated disclosure form","Waiting 5 years before replacing"],"answer":3,"explain":"The requirements are the notice, informing the existing insurer, and the state form. There is no 5-year wait.","topic":"Replacement"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"An applicant pays the first premium with the application and receives a conditional receipt. She dies before the policy is issued, and the underwriter finds she met the insurer's insurability standards.","q":"What is the likely result?","choices":["No coverage, since the policy was not issued","Coverage applies back to the date set by the receipt, since she was insurable","Only a premium refund","Coverage only after delivery"],"answer":1,"explain":"A conditional receipt gives coverage if the applicant proves insurable, effective from the receipt date.","topic":"Premium receipts"},{"situation":"A producer wants a client to cancel an existing life policy and buy a new one from the producer's company.","q":"What must the producer do?","choices":["Nothing special; it is a normal sale","Give a Notice Regarding Replacement, inform the existing insurer, and use the state disclosure form","Backdate the new policy","Wait for the free look to expire"],"answer":1,"explain":"Those are the replacement requirements: the notice, informing the existing insurer, and the state-mandated disclosure form.","topic":"Replacement"},{"situation":"A healthy applicant who goes skydiving every weekend applies for life coverage.","q":"How might the underwriter classify the applicant?","choices":["Preferred automatically","Substandard, since hazardous hobbies can lower the class","Declined only","Standard with no ratings"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hazardous hobbies such as skydiving can make a healthy person substandard.","topic":"Risk classes"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Conditional receipt","match":"Coverage only if applicant proves insurable"},{"term":"Binding receipt","match":"Coverage guaranteed until insurer rejects application"},{"term":"MIB","match":"Shared database of health information from past applications"},{"term":"Substandard","match":"Higher risk; higher premium or rejection"},{"term":"Backdating","match":"Earlier effective date to use a lower age"},{"term":"Statement of good health","match":"Confirms health unchanged when premium paid at delivery"}]},"ch05_1":{"title":"Chapter 5.1 - Life Insurance (Types of Policies)","flashcards":[{"front":"Term life insurance basics?","back":"Pure death protection for a set period. Pays only if the insured dies during the term. No cash value. Also called temporary insurance.","topic":"Term","memory":""},{"front":"Level vs decreasing vs increasing term?","back":"Level: benefit and premium stay the same. Decreasing: benefit falls to $0, premium level (mortgage protection). Increasing: benefit grows, premium rises.","topic":"Term","memory":""},{"front":"Renewable vs convertible term?","back":"Renewable: keep term coverage without proof of insurability, premium rises. Convertible: switch to permanent without proof of insurability.","topic":"Term","memory":"Renewable = same coverage, pay more; Convertible = switch, no health questions"},{"front":"Attained-age vs original-age conversion?","back":"Attained-age uses age at conversion: higher premium, no extra payment. Original-age uses issue age: lower premium, may require a payment for the difference.","topic":"Term","memory":""},{"front":"Whole life characteristics?","back":"Fixed death benefit, fixed premium, guaranteed cash value growth, lifetime protection. Cash value equals face amount at age 100.","topic":"Whole life","memory":""},{"front":"Single premium whole life tax status?","back":"One lump sum at issue, immediate cash value. Always a modified endowment contract (MEC).","topic":"Whole life","memory":""},{"front":"Endowment policy?","back":"Cash value grows fast so it matures before age 100. Pays a death benefit during the period or the face amount at maturity if alive.","topic":"Endowment","memory":""},{"front":"Family income vs family maintenance?","back":"Income: whole life plus decreasing term; income runs from the policy start date. Maintenance: whole life plus level term; lump sum plus income from the date of death.","topic":"Family policies","memory":"INCOME from the START; MAINTENANCE from the DEATH"},{"front":"First-to-die vs second-to-die?","back":"First-to-die pays at the first insured's death. Second-to-die (survivorship) pays at the last death, common in estate planning.","topic":"Joint life","memory":""},{"front":"Is universal life term or permanent?","back":"Permanent, with a cash value account, flexible premiums and an adjustable death benefit. It is not a term policy.","topic":"Universal life","memory":"UL = permanent + flexible"},{"front":"UL death benefit Option A vs Option B?","back":"Option A (level): stated amount, so pure insurance shrinks as cash value grows. Option B (increasing): face amount plus cash value.","topic":"Universal life","memory":""},{"front":"Who bears investment risk in variable life?","back":"The policyowner bears all investment risk. Agents need a life license and a FINRA registered representative license.","topic":"Variable products","memory":""},{"front":"What is a MEC and the 7-pay test?","back":"A policy overfunded under IRS rules. Premiums in the first 7 years must not exceed the amount needed to pay up the policy in 7 level premiums. Once a MEC, always a MEC.","topic":"MEC","memory":""},{"front":"MEC tax treatment?","back":"Withdrawals and loans are taxed LIFO (gains first), plus a 10 percent penalty before age 59 and a half.","topic":"MEC","memory":"MEC = Money Early Cost"},{"front":"Participating vs nonparticipating?","back":"Participating policies pay dividends (reduce premiums, buy more coverage, or take cash). Nonparticipating pay none.","topic":"Dividends","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which type of policy provides death protection for a set period with no cash value?","choices":["Whole life","Term life","Universal life","Endowment"],"answer":1,"explain":"Term is temporary protection with no cash value.","topic":"Term"},{"q":"A term policy whose death benefit falls on a set schedule to $0 while the premium stays level is:","choices":["Level term","Increasing term","Decreasing term","Convertible term"],"answer":2,"explain":"Decreasing term is used for mortgage protection and credit life.","topic":"Term"},{"q":"Universal life is best described as:","choices":["Permanent with flexible premiums","Temporary with cash value","Term with a fixed premium","A nonparticipating endowment"],"answer":0,"explain":"UL is permanent, not term, with flexible premiums.","topic":"Universal life"},{"q":"A policy that pays nothing on death and pays only if the insured survives is a:","choices":["Semi-endowment","Pure endowment","Juvenile endowment","Family plan"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pure endowment pays only on survival. Semi-endowment pays half on survival and full on death.","topic":"Endowment"},{"q":"The 7-pay test applies to determining whether a policy is:","choices":["Participating","A MEC","Convertible","Renewable"],"answer":1,"explain":"Failing the 7-pay test makes the policy a MEC.","topic":"MEC"},{"q":"A MEC withdrawal before age 59 and a half is generally taxed:","choices":["FIFO, no penalty","LIFO with a 10 percent penalty","Never taxed","Only at death"],"answer":1,"explain":"MEC distributions are LIFO (gains first) and carry a 10 percent penalty before 59 and a half.","topic":"MEC"},{"q":"Face amount of credit life insurance can be no more than:","choices":["Twice the debt","Half the debt","The outstanding debt","$50,000"],"answer":2,"explain":"Credit life covers a loan balance; the term matches the loan and the face is no more than the debt.","topic":"Term"},{"q":"Which death benefit option equals the face amount plus the cash value?","choices":["Option A","Option B","Corridor option","Level option"],"answer":1,"explain":"Option B is increasing. Option A is level, with pure insurance shrinking as cash value grows.","topic":"Universal life"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of term life?","choices":["Lower initial premiums","Builds guaranteed cash value","Premiums rise on renewal","Can protect insurability with renewable and convertible options"],"answer":1,"explain":"Term has no cash value.","topic":"Term"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of variable life?","choices":["Policyowner bears investment risk","Regulated by state insurance departments and the SEC","Agent needs a life license and FINRA registration","Has a guaranteed minimum cash value"],"answer":3,"explain":"Variable whole life has no guaranteed interest rate or minimum cash value.","topic":"Variable products"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of whole life?","choices":["Fixed premium","Fixed death benefit","Flexible premiums set by the owner","Cash value equals face at age 100"],"answer":2,"explain":"Flexible premiums describe universal life, not whole life.","topic":"Whole life"},{"q":"Which is NOT a feature of the family income policy?","choices":["Whole life plus decreasing term","Monthly income if death occurs in the set period","Lump sum plus income from the date of death","Payments run from the policy start date"],"answer":2,"explain":"Lump sum plus income from date of death describes family maintenance.","topic":"Family policies"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"A 30-year-old buys a 20-year level term policy. In year 12 her health worsens and she wants permanent coverage without a medical exam. Her policy has a conversion option.","q":"What option allows this?","choices":["Renewal","Conversion to a permanent policy without evidence of insurability","Re-entry","Single premium purchase"],"answer":1,"explain":"Convertible term lets the insured switch to permanent coverage without proving insurability.","topic":"Term"},{"situation":"A couple wants to pay estate taxes at the death of the last surviving spouse and wants a lower premium than two separate policies.","q":"Which policy fits best?","choices":["First-to-die","Second-to-die (survivorship)","Family maintenance","Jumping juvenile"],"answer":1,"explain":"Survivorship pays at the last death and is common in estate planning.","topic":"Joint life"},{"situation":"A policyowner funds a whole life policy with unusually large premiums in the first few years, exceeding the 7-pay limit. Later she takes a loan from the cash value at age 50.","q":"What is the tax result?","choices":["Loan is tax-free as a policy loan","Loan is taxed LIFO as gain with a 10 percent penalty","Loan is taxed FIFO with no penalty","Policy converts back to normal life insurance"],"answer":1,"explain":"Failing the 7-pay test makes it a MEC. MEC loans are taxed LIFO with a 10 percent penalty before 59 and a half.","topic":"MEC"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Decreasing term","match":"Death benefit falls to zero; used for mortgage protection"},{"term":"Attained-age conversion","match":"Premium based on age at conversion"},{"term":"Pure endowment","match":"Pays only if the insured survives to maturity"},{"term":"Family maintenance","match":"Lump sum plus income from the date of death"},{"term":"Second-to-die","match":"Pays at death of the last surviving insured"},{"term":"MEC","match":"Overfunded policy failing the 7-pay test"}]},"ch05_2":{"title":"Chapter 5 (Part 2) - Policy Provisions, Options, Riders and Exclusions","flashcards":[{"front":"Absolute vs collateral assignment","back":"Absolute assignment is a permanent, full transfer of ownership. Collateral assignment is temporary and secures a loan; the lender is paid first from proceeds.","topic":"Assignment","memory":"Absolute = all rights; Collateral = loan security"},{"front":"Incontestable clause exceptions","back":"After the 2-year period (Louisiana life: 2 years; health: 3 years) the insurer can still challenge for impersonation or identity fraud, no insurable interest, intent to murder, age or sex misstatement, and nonpayment of premiums.","topic":"Incontestable clause","memory":""},{"front":"Suicide exclusion length","back":"Generally the first 2 years. If it applies, the insurer refunds premiums paid, with no interest, instead of the death benefit.","topic":"Exclusions","memory":""},{"front":"What does reinstatement require?","back":"Application, evidence of insurability, and back premiums plus interest. It restores the original rate and coverage. A surrendered policy cannot be reinstated. Louisiana life: within 3 years of default.","topic":"Reinstatement","memory":""},{"front":"Three nonforfeiture options","back":"Cash surrender, reduced paid-up insurance, and extended term insurance. Extended term is usually the default and gives the highest face amount of the three options.","topic":"Nonforfeiture","memory":"CASH, REDUCED, EXTENDED"},{"front":"Reduced paid-up insurance","back":"Cash value buys a smaller, fully paid whole life policy that lasts for life. No proof of insurability is needed; riders are not carried over.","topic":"Nonforfeiture","memory":""},{"front":"Policy loan basics","back":"Not a traditional loan: no credit check, permanent policies only. Repayment is not required, but unpaid loans plus interest reduce the death benefit and can lapse the policy.","topic":"Policy loans","memory":""},{"front":"Insurer's right to defer","back":"Insurer may delay cash surrender or loan payments up to 6 months. It does not apply to death claims.","topic":"Cash value","memory":""},{"front":"Who receives dividends?","back":"Only participating (par) policies. Dividends are not guaranteed, are generally not taxable, and a producer may never guarantee them.","topic":"Dividends","memory":""},{"front":"Dividend option that maximizes death coverage","back":"Paid-up additions (PUA). They buy small amounts of paid-up insurance with no proof of insurability.","topic":"Dividends","memory":""},{"front":"Waiver of premium rider","back":"Insurer pays premiums if the insured is totally disabled after a waiting period (6 months). It does not pay the insured income and expires around age 60-65.","topic":"Riders","memory":"Waives premium, pays no income"},{"front":"Accidental death benefit (ADB) rider","back":"Pays double or triple the face amount if accidental death occurs within 90 days of the accident. Excludes self-injury, war and similar risks.","topic":"Riders","memory":""},{"front":"AD&D principal sum vs capital sum","back":"Principal sum is the amount paid for accidental death. Capital sum is paid for dismemberment or loss of sight and is usually a portion, often half.","topic":"Riders","memory":""},{"front":"Long-term care rider trigger","back":"Benefits typically start when the insured cannot perform 2 of the 6 activities of daily living: bathing, continence, dressing, eating, toileting, transferring.","topic":"Living benefits","memory":"B-C-D-E-T-T"},{"front":"Revocable vs irrevocable beneficiary","back":"Owner can change a revocable beneficiary anytime without consent. An irrevocable beneficiary has a vested interest and must consent to changes.","topic":"Beneficiaries","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"An assignment that permanently transfers all ownership rights in a policy is called:","choices":["Collateral assignment","Absolute assignment","Beneficiary designation","Policy reinstatement"],"answer":1,"explain":"Absolute assignment is a full, permanent transfer. Collateral assignment is only temporary security for a loan.","topic":"Assignment"},{"q":"Which nonforfeiture option provides the highest face amount, though only for a limited time?","choices":["Cash surrender","Reduced paid-up","Extended term","Automatic premium loan"],"answer":2,"explain":"Extended term uses the cash value as a single premium for term coverage, usually for the same face amount. Reduced paid-up gives a smaller face amount for life.","topic":"Nonforfeiture"},{"q":"Dividends are paid only on which type of policy?","choices":["Nonparticipating","Participating","Term only","Group credit"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only participating (par) policies pay dividends, and they are not guaranteed.","topic":"Dividends"},{"q":"A rider that pays double or triple the face amount for death by accident is the:","choices":["Accidental death benefit rider","Waiver of premium rider","Guaranteed insurability option","Cost of living rider"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ADB rider pays a multiple of face for accidental death. Waiver of premium only covers premiums during disability.","topic":"Riders"},{"q":"How long may an insurer generally delay paying a cash surrender or loan request under the right to defer?","choices":["30 days","90 days","6 months","2 years"],"answer":2,"explain":"The right to defer is up to 6 months. It does not apply to death claims.","topic":"Cash value"},{"q":"Under the standard suicide exclusion, what does the insurer typically pay if the insured dies by suicide in the first 2 years?","choices":["Full face amount","Premiums paid, without interest","Cash value plus interest","Nothing at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"The insurer refunds the premiums paid, no interest, instead of the death benefit. After the first 2 years the death benefit is paid.","topic":"Exclusions"},{"q":"The guaranteed insurability option (GIO) allows the owner to:","choices":["Convert term to whole life without a medical exam","Buy additional coverage at specified times without evidence of insurability","Receive the face amount early if terminally ill","Skip premiums during disability"],"answer":1,"explain":"GIO gives the right to buy more insurance at set dates or events with no medical exam. Unused options do not roll over.","topic":"Riders"},{"q":"Which disability rider benefit does the waiver of premium rider provide?","choices":["Monthly income to the insured","Payment of the policy premiums while totally disabled","A lump sum of the face amount","Reduced premiums for life"],"answer":1,"explain":"It waives premiums only. Income during disability comes from a disability income rider.","topic":"Riders"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a daily living activity used for long-term care benefit triggers?","choices":["Bathing","Toileting","Driving","Transferring"],"answer":2,"explain":"The six standard ADLs are bathing, continence, dressing, eating, toileting and transferring. Driving is not one of them.","topic":"Living benefits"},{"q":"Which is NOT an exception to the incontestable clause?","choices":["Impersonation of the insured","No insurable interest","A misstatement of medical history discovered after the contestable period","Intent to murder the insured"],"answer":2,"explain":"After the contestable period the insurer generally cannot contest for application misstatements. The other three remain valid grounds.","topic":"Incontestable clause"},{"q":"Which statement about policy loans is NOT true?","choices":["No credit check is required","Unpaid loans reduce the death benefit","Loan interest on personal policies is tax deductible","They are available on permanent policies"],"answer":2,"explain":"Interest on a personal policy loan is not deductible. The other statements are true.","topic":"Policy loans"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a dividend option?","choices":["Cash","Paid-up additions","Accumulate at interest","Automatic increase of the face amount by CPI"],"answer":3,"explain":"Standard options are cash, premium reduction, paid-up additions, one-year term, accumulation at interest and paid-up. Interest on accumulated dividends is taxable.","topic":"Dividends"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Maria stops paying premiums on her whole life policy and does not choose any nonforfeiture option. The policy has no outstanding loan.","q":"What happens by default?","choices":["The policy is surrendered for cash","Extended term insurance for the same face amount begins","Reduced paid-up insurance is issued","The policy is canceled with no value"],"answer":1,"explain":"Extended term is the default nonforfeiture option, typically at the same face amount if there is no loan. Cash surrender must be requested.","topic":"Nonforfeiture"},{"situation":"Carlos has a policy with a 2x accidental death benefit rider that requires death within 90 days of the accident. He is injured in a car crash and dies 120 days later of those injuries.","q":"What is paid?","choices":["Double the face amount","Triple the face amount","Only the base face amount, not the double","Nothing"],"answer":2,"explain":"Death fell outside the 90-day window so the ADB rider does not pay. The base policy still pays the face amount.","topic":"Riders"},{"situation":"Priya names her sister as beneficiary on her policy without making the designation irrevocable. Later she marries and wants to name her husband instead.","q":"What must she do?","choices":["Get her sister's written consent","Name the husband without her sister's consent","Wait until the contestable period ends","Assign the policy absolutely"],"answer":1,"explain":"A revocable beneficiary has no vested interest, so the owner may change the designation anytime. Only an irrevocable beneficiary must consent.","topic":"Beneficiaries"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Absolute assignment","match":"Permanent transfer of all ownership rights"},{"term":"Collateral assignment","match":"Temporary transfer securing a loan"},{"term":"Extended term","match":"Usual default option; highest face amount, for a limited time"},{"term":"Paid-up additions","match":"Dividend option that maximizes death coverage"},{"term":"Waiver of premium","match":"Rider that pays premiums during total disability, no income"},{"term":"Capital sum","match":"AD&D amount for dismemberment or loss of sight"}]},"ch06":{"title":"Chapter 6 - Group Life Insurance","flashcards":[{"front":"Master policy vs certificate of insurance","back":"The employer (policyholder) owns the master policy. Each insured employee receives a certificate of insurance as evidence of coverage.","topic":"Group basics","memory":"Master = employer; Certificate = employee"},{"front":"Noncontributory group plan","back":"The employer pays 100% of the premium, and 100% of eligible employees must participate.","topic":"Contributory vs noncontributory","memory":"Non = Nothing paid by employee"},{"front":"Contributory group plan","back":"Employees share the cost with the employer. At least 75% of eligible employees must participate.","topic":"Contributory vs noncontributory","memory":""},{"front":"Group conversion privilege","back":"On leaving the group, an employee may convert to an individual policy, usually whole life, without proof of insurability, within 31 days.","topic":"Conversion","memory":"31 days, no evidence"},{"front":"Conversion premium basis","back":"The converted individual policy is priced at the employee's attained age, not the original age.","topic":"Conversion","memory":""},{"front":"Death during the 31-day conversion window","back":"If the employee dies within the 31 days before converting, the group policy pays the death benefit.","topic":"Conversion","memory":""},{"front":"When the master policy is terminated","back":"Members insured under it for 5 or more years may convert, up to the original face amount.","topic":"Conversion","memory":""},{"front":"Adverse selection in group insurance","back":"The tendency of unhealthy people to seek coverage. Group plans counter it with minimum participation and by covering the group, not individuals.","topic":"Underwriting","memory":""},{"front":"Proof of insurability in large groups","back":"Usually not required for large groups. Small groups may require it, and late enrollees may need evidence.","topic":"Underwriting","memory":""},{"front":"Employee enrollment window","back":"Eligible employees typically have 31 days to enroll after becoming eligible. Probation periods commonly run 1 to 6 months.","topic":"Eligibility","memory":""},{"front":"Tax treatment of employer-paid group term premiums","back":"The first $50,000 of group term coverage is tax-free to the employee. Cost for coverage above $50,000 may be taxable income.","topic":"Taxation","memory":"Fifty thousand free"},{"front":"Credit life insurance","back":"Pays a lender if a borrower dies. Usually decreasing term, with the creditor as beneficiary, and the premium is not based on age.","topic":"Special group types","memory":""},{"front":"Franchise insurance","back":"Individual policies sold to members of a group at wholesale or reduced rates, each underwritten individually.","topic":"Special group types","memory":""},{"front":"Blanket insurance","back":"Covers a group exposed to the same hazard, such as airline passengers, with no individual names and no certificates issued.","topic":"Special group types","memory":""},{"front":"Incident of ownership in group life","back":"The employee holds the incident of ownership, such as naming the beneficiary, even though the employer holds the master policy.","topic":"Ownership","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"In a group life plan, who owns the master policy?","choices":["Each employee","The beneficiary","The employer or policyholder","The state insurance department"],"answer":2,"explain":"The group policyholder, usually the employer, owns the master policy. Employees receive certificates.","topic":"Group basics"},{"q":"A group plan in which employees pay part of the premium is called:","choices":["Noncontributory","Contributory","Franchise","Blanket"],"answer":1,"explain":"Contributory means employees share the cost. In a noncontributory plan the employer pays all of it.","topic":"Contributory vs noncontributory"},{"q":"Which kind of insurance is sold to a group with no individual names and no certificates?","choices":["Franchise","Credit life","Blanket","Contributory group"],"answer":2,"explain":"Blanket coverage insures a group with common exposure and issues no certificates. Franchise insurance is individually underwritten.","topic":"Special group types"},{"q":"Group credit life insurance is generally written as which type of coverage?","choices":["Whole life","Decreasing term","Universal life","Endowment"],"answer":1,"explain":"Credit life tracks a shrinking loan balance, so it is decreasing term payable to the creditor.","topic":"Special group types"},{"q":"What percentage of eligible employees must participate in a noncontributory group plan?","choices":["50%","75%","85%","100%"],"answer":3,"explain":"Noncontributory plans require 100% participation because the employer pays everything. Contributory plans require 75%.","topic":"Contributory vs noncontributory"},{"q":"Within how many days after termination of employment must an employee apply to convert group coverage?","choices":["10","31","60","90"],"answer":1,"explain":"The conversion privilege lasts 31 days and needs no proof of insurability.","topic":"Conversion"},{"q":"How much employer-paid group term coverage is generally tax-free to the employee?","choices":["$10,000","$25,000","$50,000","$100,000"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first $50,000 is tax-free. Coverage above that may create taxable income.","topic":"Taxation"},{"q":"Which statement about group conversion is NOT true?","choices":["No proof of insurability is required","Premium is based on attained age","The employee may keep the original group rate","It usually converts to individual whole life"],"answer":2,"explain":"The converted policy is priced at attained age in the individual market, not at the group rate.","topic":"Conversion"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT true of group underwriting?","choices":["Large groups usually need no proof of insurability","The group as a whole is evaluated","A physical impairment automatically excludes an employee","Adverse selection is a concern"],"answer":2,"explain":"Physical impairments do not exclude an eligible employee from group coverage.","topic":"Underwriting"},{"q":"Which is NOT a requirement for an insured group?","choices":["The group was formed for a purpose other than buying insurance","The group meets any state minimum size","Members are individually medically underwritten","A minimum participation percentage is met"],"answer":2,"explain":"Group coverage is underwritten on the group, not each member. Individual underwriting describes franchise insurance.","topic":"Eligibility"},{"q":"Which statement about taxation of group life is NOT correct?","choices":["Employer premiums are generally deductible","Employee-paid premiums are not deductible","Lump-sum death benefit is generally income-tax-free","Interest on installment payments is tax-free"],"answer":3,"explain":"Interest paid with installments is taxable. The other statements are correct.","topic":"Taxation"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of franchise insurance?","choices":["Individual policies are issued","It is sold at wholesale rates","Members get certificates under one master policy","It is individually underwritten"],"answer":2,"explain":"Franchise insurance uses individual policies, not a master policy and certificates.","topic":"Special group types"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Dana leaves her job and her group life coverage ends on the last day of the month. She has some health problems and wants to keep life insurance.","q":"What is her best option?","choices":["She has no option because she is uninsurable","Apply for conversion to an individual policy within 31 days without proof of insurability","Wait 60 days then reapply to the group","Ask the employer to continue the master policy for her alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"The conversion privilege is available within 31 days with no medical evidence. Premium is at her attained age.","topic":"Conversion"},{"situation":"An employer pays the entire premium for group term life for all employees. Only 80% of eligible employees are enrolled, and the rest declined.","q":"Does the plan meet standard participation rules for this type?","choices":["Yes, 75% is enough","Yes, 50% is enough","No, a noncontributory plan needs 100% participation","No, it must be contributory"],"answer":2,"explain":"In a noncontributory plan the employer pays everything, so 100% of eligible employees must be covered. 75% applies to contributory plans.","topic":"Contributory vs noncontributory"},{"situation":"Jordan takes out a car loan and the lender offers a policy that will pay off the loan balance if Jordan dies. Premium does not depend on Jordan's age.","q":"What is this coverage and who is the beneficiary?","choices":["Franchise life, with Jordan's spouse as beneficiary","Credit life, with the lender as beneficiary","Blanket life, with Jordan's estate as beneficiary","Whole life, with the employer as beneficiary"],"answer":1,"explain":"Credit life pays the creditor the outstanding balance, usually through decreasing term.","topic":"Special group types"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Contributory","match":"Employees share cost; 75% participation required"},{"term":"Noncontributory","match":"Employer pays all; 100% participation required"},{"term":"Master policy","match":"Contract issued to the employer, the policyholder"},{"term":"Certificate of insurance","match":"Evidence of coverage given to each employee"},{"term":"Franchise insurance","match":"Individual policies sold to group members at wholesale"},{"term":"Blanket insurance","match":"Group with common exposure; no names, no certificates"}]},"ch07":{"title":"Chapter 7 - Premiums, Proceeds, Settlement Options and Beneficiaries","flashcards":[{"front":"Three factors that set a life insurance premium","back":"Mortality, interest and expenses. Mortality is the most significant. Higher mortality or expenses raise the premium; higher interest lowers it.","topic":"Premium factors","memory":"MIE: Mortality, Interest, Expenses"},{"front":"Net premium vs gross premium","back":"Net premium covers mortality cost and interest only. Gross premium is net premium plus expenses (loading) and is what the policyholder pays.","topic":"Premium basics","memory":"Gross = Net + Expenses"},{"front":"Premium mode, cheapest to costliest","back":"Annual is cheapest, then semiannual, quarterly and monthly. More frequent payments cost more in total.","topic":"Premium mode","memory":""},{"front":"Earned vs unearned premium","back":"Earned premium is the part for coverage already provided. Unearned premium is the part for coverage not yet provided.","topic":"Reserves","memory":""},{"front":"Surrender cost index","back":"Average net cost per $1,000 if the policy is surrendered for cash at a set point, such as 10 or 20 years. Suits cash value focused buyers.","topic":"Cost comparison","memory":""},{"front":"Net payment cost index","back":"Average annual premium outlay assuming the policy is not surrendered. Excludes cash value, so it suits death benefit focused buyers.","topic":"Cost comparison","memory":""},{"front":"Comparative interest rate (CIR) method","back":"Compares buying term and investing the difference. Finds the return the investment needs to match the cash value policy. A higher CIR favors the permanent policy.","topic":"Cost comparison","memory":""},{"front":"Accelerated death benefit","back":"Living benefit paying part of the face amount early to a terminally ill insured, usually up to about 50%. Generally tax-free when terminally ill.","topic":"Living benefits","memory":""},{"front":"Viatical settlement vs life settlement","back":"Viatical: seller is chronically or terminally ill. Life settlement: no illness required, and the seller gets more than cash surrender value but less than the death benefit.","topic":"Settlements","memory":""},{"front":"Five settlement options","back":"Lump sum (default), interest only, fixed amount, fixed period, and life income.","topic":"Settlement options","memory":""},{"front":"Fixed amount vs fixed period option","back":"Fixed amount focuses on the dollar income; the payout lasts until funds run out. Fixed period focuses on time, ignoring life expectancy.","topic":"Settlement options","memory":"Amount = dollars; Period = time"},{"front":"Life income sub-options","back":"Single (pure) life pays most, no refund. Refund guarantees at least principal. Period certain guarantees years. Joint and survivor covers two lives.","topic":"Settlement options","memory":""},{"front":"Revocable vs irrevocable beneficiary","back":"Owner may change a revocable beneficiary anytime. Changing an irrevocable beneficiary needs that beneficiary's consent, and that beneficiary has a vested interest.","topic":"Beneficiaries","memory":""},{"front":"Per capita vs per stirpes","back":"Per capita splits equally among surviving class members. Per stirpes lets a deceased member's share pass to that member's descendants.","topic":"Beneficiaries","memory":"Per stirpes = by the branch"},{"front":"Income tax on life insurance proceeds","back":"Premiums are not deductible. Lump-sum death benefit is generally income-tax-free. Installment interest is taxable. Policy loans are generally not taxable, except for a MEC.","topic":"Taxation","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which factor is the most significant in setting the premium for life insurance?","choices":["Expenses","Interest","Mortality","Reserves"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mortality is the most significant premium factor.","topic":"Premium factors"},{"q":"Gross premium equals:","choices":["Net premium minus expenses","Net premium plus expenses","Mortality minus interest only","Cash value plus dividends"],"answer":1,"explain":"Gross premium adds the expense loading to the net premium.","topic":"Premium basics"},{"q":"Which settlement option pays benefits for as long as the beneficiary lives?","choices":["Fixed period","Interest only","Fixed amount","Life income"],"answer":3,"explain":"Life income is based on life expectancy and cannot be outlived. Fixed period ends after the chosen years.","topic":"Settlement options"},{"q":"Who may be named when the policyholder wants coverage on a group like 'all my children'?","choices":["A tertiary beneficiary","A class beneficiary","A contingent owner","An irrevocable assignee"],"answer":1,"explain":"A class beneficiary is a group; new members such as births and adoptions are added automatically.","topic":"Beneficiaries"},{"q":"Which premium mode costs the policyholder the least over a year?","choices":["Monthly","Quarterly","Semiannual","Annual"],"answer":3,"explain":"Annual payment is cheapest overall. Monthly is the most expensive.","topic":"Premium mode"},{"q":"What is the default settlement option when none is chosen?","choices":["Interest only","Fixed amount","Lump sum","Life income"],"answer":2,"explain":"Lump sum is the most common option and the default.","topic":"Settlement options"},{"q":"Under per stirpes with three children, one deceased child who left two children, how much does each surviving child receive (exact)?","choices":["One-half","One-quarter","One-third","One-sixth"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each of the three branches takes one-third. The deceased child's two children share one-third, so each gets one-sixth.","topic":"Beneficiaries"},{"q":"Which index is best for a buyer who plans to surrender the policy in 10 to 20 years?","choices":["Net payment cost index","Surrender cost index","Loss ratio","Persistency ratio"],"answer":1,"explain":"The surrender cost index assumes the policy is surrendered. The net payment cost index assumes it is not.","topic":"Cost comparison"},{"q":"Which statement about premium factors is NOT correct?","choices":["Higher mortality raises premium","Higher expenses raise premium","Higher interest earnings raise premium","Interest earned reduces the premium needed"],"answer":2,"explain":"Higher interest earnings lower the premium, not raise it.","topic":"Premium factors"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of an irrevocable beneficiary?","choices":["Has a vested interest","May receive a copy of the policy","Must consent to a policy loan","Can be changed by the owner at any time without consent"],"answer":3,"explain":"Changes to an irrevocable beneficiary require that beneficiary's consent.","topic":"Beneficiaries"},{"q":"Which is NOT a feature of a viatical settlement?","choices":["Seller is called the viator","Seller must be terminally or chronically ill","Seller receives less than the death benefit","Seller is paid the full death benefit"],"answer":3,"explain":"The viator receives less than the death benefit, since the buyer takes the death benefit later.","topic":"Settlements"},{"q":"Which statement about taxation of life insurance is NOT correct?","choices":["Premiums for personal policies are not deductible","Lump-sum proceeds are generally income-tax-free","Interest paid on installments is tax-free","A 1035 exchange is generally not taxable"],"answer":2,"explain":"Interest earned on installments is taxable; the principal portion is not.","topic":"Taxation"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"A physician certifies that Tom, who has a terminal illness, is expected to live less than two years. Tom needs money for care and owns a $200,000 policy with an accelerated benefit provision.","q":"What is the usual result?","choices":["He can receive part of the face amount early, often up to about 50%, generally tax-free","He can receive 100% plus dividends tax-free","He must wait until death to claim","He must surrender the policy and pay tax on the full gain"],"answer":0,"explain":"Accelerated benefits pay part of the face while the insured is alive, commonly capped around 50%, with the remainder paid at death.","topic":"Living benefits"},{"situation":"The insured's policy names three adult children as beneficiaries. One child dies before the insured and leaves two children. The policy says per stirpes.","q":"How is the death benefit divided?","choices":["Each of the two surviving children gets one-half","Each of the two surviving children gets one-third, and the grandchildren share one-third","The grandchildren get nothing","Everything goes to the estate"],"answer":1,"explain":"Per stirpes keeps each branch's share. The two grandchildren split the deceased child's one-third.","topic":"Beneficiaries"},{"situation":"A beneficiary wants predictable payments of $2,000 a month until the proceeds are used up, not a set number of years and not for life.","q":"Which option fits?","choices":["Fixed period","Interest only","Fixed amount","Joint and survivor"],"answer":2,"explain":"Fixed amount sets the payment and runs until principal and interest are exhausted. Fixed period sets the time instead.","topic":"Settlement options"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Gross premium","match":"Net premium plus expenses"},{"term":"Surrender cost index","match":"Average net cost per $1,000 if policy is surrendered"},{"term":"Fixed period option","match":"Equal payments over a chosen number of years"},{"term":"Single life income","match":"Highest lifetime income, no refund at death"},{"term":"Contingent beneficiary","match":"Receives benefit if the primary dies first"},{"term":"Life settlement","match":"Sale of a policy to a third party, no illness needed"}]},"ch08":{"title":"Chapter 8 - Uses of Life Insurance and Annuity Basics","flashcards":[{"front":"Five steps of financial planning","back":"Gather information, identify objectives, analyze finances, develop the plan, and review it periodically.","topic":"Planning","memory":""},{"front":"Human life value approach","back":"Annual earned income minus expenses, multiplied by years to retirement. It does not account for others who financially benefit from the insured's life.","topic":"Needs analysis","memory":""},{"front":"Needs approach","back":"Considered the most thorough method. Adds up lump-sum needs and income needs, then subtracts existing liquid assets.","topic":"Needs analysis","memory":"NEEDS = Lump sums + Income gap - Liquid assets"},{"front":"Social Security blackout period","back":"The time from the insured's death until the spouse is eligible for retirement benefits. Child benefits continue until age 18.","topic":"Needs analysis","memory":""},{"front":"Key person insurance","back":"Covers an employee whose death would hurt the business. The business is applicant, owner, premium payor and beneficiary. Premiums are generally not deductible.","topic":"Business uses","memory":""},{"front":"Purpose of a buy-sell agreement","back":"Funds the purchase of a deceased owner's share of the business, so the survivors can buy it and the family receives cash.","topic":"Business uses","memory":""},{"front":"Entity vs cross-purchase buy-sell","back":"In an entity plan the business owns one policy per owner. In a cross-purchase each owner buys policies on every other owner.","topic":"Buy-sell","memory":""},{"front":"Number of policies in cross-purchase","back":"n x (n - 1). Three partners need 6 policies; four partners need 12. An entity plan needs one per owner.","topic":"Buy-sell","memory":"Each buys one on every other partner"},{"front":"Deferred compensation vs salary continuation","back":"Deferred compensation is non-qualified and may be funded by cash value life or annuities. Salary continuation is fully employer-funded under a formal agreement.","topic":"Employer plans","memory":""},{"front":"Executive bonus (Section 162) plan","back":"Employer pays a bonus used for the employee's policy. Bonus is deductible to the employer and taxable to the employee, who controls the policy.","topic":"Employer plans","memory":""},{"front":"Corporate-owned life insurance (COLI) tax basics","back":"Death proceeds are generally income-tax-free to the corporation, subject to notice and consent rules. The $50,000 group-term rule does not apply to business-owned life insurance.","topic":"COLI","memory":""},{"front":"Split-dollar","back":"A method of sharing a policy's cost and benefits between employer and employee. It is an arrangement, not a type of policy.","topic":"Employer plans","memory":""},{"front":"Annuity exclusion ratio","back":"The portion of each annuity payment that is a tax-free return of the investment, with the rest taxable.","topic":"Annuities","memory":""},{"front":"1035 exchange","back":"Moves an annuity or life policy to another contract without immediate tax on the gain; tax is deferred, not eliminated.","topic":"Annuities","memory":""},{"front":"Variable annuity licensing","back":"Requires both a securities license (FINRA) and a state insurance license, since it is a security.","topic":"Annuities","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which approach to determining life insurance amount is considered the best?","choices":["Human life value","Needs approach","Multiple earnings","Seat of the pants"],"answer":1,"explain":"The needs approach looks at actual family needs and subtracts existing assets, so it is most thorough.","topic":"Needs analysis"},{"q":"Key person life insurance is purchased on:","choices":["The business owner's spouse","An employee valuable to the business","A competitor","A customer"],"answer":1,"explain":"The business insures the key employee and receives the benefit if that person dies.","topic":"Business uses"},{"q":"A buy-sell agreement funded with life insurance is intended to:","choices":["Provide the funds to buy a deceased owner's interest","Reduce the business's income taxes","Pay the owner a retirement income","Insure a key employee"],"answer":0,"explain":"The insurance provides the funds for the survivors or the business to buy the deceased owner's share from the estate.","topic":"Buy-sell"},{"q":"Who is the annuitant in an annuity?","choices":["The company issuing the contract","The person whose life determines payments","The person who must pay tax on death","The agent"],"answer":1,"explain":"The annuitant's life sets the length of payments. The owner and annuitant can be different people.","topic":"Annuities"},{"q":"How many policies are needed for a cross-purchase agreement among three partners?","choices":["3","6","9","12"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each of 3 partners buys a policy on each of the other 2, so 3 x 2 = 6.","topic":"Buy-sell"},{"q":"How many policies does an entity (stock redemption) plan need for four owners?","choices":["4","6","8","12"],"answer":0,"explain":"The business buys one policy on each owner. Cross-purchase would need 12 for four owners.","topic":"Buy-sell"},{"q":"Federal estate tax on the estate of a decedent is generally due:","choices":["Within 6 months of death","9 months after death","Within 30 days of death","At the time of filing the next income tax return"],"answer":1,"explain":"The estate tax return and payment are generally due 9 months after death. State rules can differ.","topic":"Estate planning"},{"q":"The first $50,000 tax-free rule applies to which kind of coverage?","choices":["Corporate-owned life insurance (COLI)","Group term life insurance","Key person life","Split-dollar"],"answer":1,"explain":"The $50,000 figure comes from the group term rule. It does not apply to business-owned life insurance such as COLI.","topic":"Taxation"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of key person insurance?","choices":["The business is the beneficiary","The business owns the policy","Premiums are generally not deductible","The insured employee's family is the beneficiary"],"answer":3,"explain":"The business is the beneficiary, since it suffers the loss.","topic":"Business uses"},{"q":"Which is NOT a step in the needs approach?","choices":["Identify lump-sum needs","Estimate income needs","Subtract liquid assets","Multiply income by the years to retirement"],"answer":3,"explain":"Multiplying income by years to retirement is the human life value method.","topic":"Needs analysis"},{"q":"Which statement about variable annuities is NOT true?","choices":["They need a FINRA license to sell","They need a state insurance license to sell","They are insurance products only and not securities","Owners bear investment risk"],"answer":2,"explain":"Variable annuities are securities as well as insurance products, which is why both licenses are needed.","topic":"Annuities"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of split-dollar?","choices":["It is an arrangement for sharing cost and benefits","It is a type of policy","Employer and employee may share premiums","It is a method of financing"],"answer":1,"explain":"Split-dollar is a method of financing, not a policy type.","topic":"Employer plans"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Three partners want to fund a buy-sell agreement where each partner buys a policy on each of the others. Partner A dies.","q":"How many policies were purchased, and who receives the proceeds on Partner A's life?","choices":["3 policies; the business","6 policies; the surviving partners who own those policies","6 policies; Partner A's family","9 policies; the partnership"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cross-purchase with three partners needs 6 policies. Surviving partners own and collect on policies on A, then buy A's share.","topic":"Buy-sell"},{"situation":"A corporation buys a policy on its top salesperson and names itself as owner and beneficiary. The salesperson's departure or death would seriously reduce sales.","q":"What is this arrangement?","choices":["Executive bonus plan","Key person insurance","Deferred compensation","Cross-purchase agreement"],"answer":1,"explain":"The corporation is the owner, premium payor and beneficiary on an employee's life, which is key person coverage.","topic":"Business uses"},{"situation":"A baker who is a sole proprietor wants to make sure that the bakery can be sold for fair value to a trusted employee if the baker dies.","q":"Which is the best fit?","choices":["A buy-sell agreement funded by life insurance","A joint and survivor annuity","Key person insurance on the employee","A group term plan"],"answer":0,"explain":"A sole proprietorship generally ends at death. A funded buy-sell agreement gives the employee money to buy the business and the family receives cash.","topic":"Buy-sell"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Human life value","match":"Income minus expenses times years to retirement"},{"term":"Key person insurance","match":"Business owns policy on valuable employee"},{"term":"Cross-purchase","match":"Each owner buys policies on the other owners"},{"term":"Entity plan","match":"Business owns one policy on each owner"},{"term":"Executive bonus","match":"Employer bonus deductible; employee owns the policy"},{"term":"Annuitant","match":"Person whose life determines annuity payments"}]},"ch09":{"title":"Chapter 9 - Annuities","flashcards":[{"front":"Life insurance vs annuity","back":"Life insurance creates an estate. An annuity liquidates one, turning savings into an income stream. Annuities have an accumulation phase and an annuity (payout) phase.","topic":"Basics","memory":""},{"front":"Fixed vs variable annuity risk","back":"Fixed annuity: general account, the insurer bears investment risk. Variable annuity: separate account, the owner bears investment risk.","topic":"Fixed vs variable","memory":""},{"front":"Variable annuity regulation","back":"Regulated by state insurance, SEC and FINRA. Requires a prospectus, and the seller needs a securities license (Series 6 or 7) plus a state insurance license.","topic":"Regulation","memory":""},{"front":"Parties to an annuity","back":"Insurer, owner, annuitant (whose life sets the payment length), and beneficiary. If no beneficiary survives, the estate receives.","topic":"Parties","memory":""},{"front":"Death benefit during accumulation","back":"Generally the contributions plus interest or accumulated value, not a face amount like life insurance.","topic":"Accumulation","memory":""},{"front":"Qualified vs non-qualified annuity","back":"Qualified uses pre-tax dollars in an employer or retirement plan. Non-qualified uses after-tax dollars. Both grow tax-deferred, but only qualified contributions are deductible.","topic":"Tax class","memory":""},{"front":"Single premium immediate annuity (SPIA)","back":"Bought with one lump sum with income starting soon after, typically within 30 days and no later than 12 months.","topic":"Timing","memory":""},{"front":"Accumulation units vs annuity units","back":"Accumulation units are bought during the savings phase. At payout the number of annuity units is fixed, but unit value varies, so payment = units x unit value.","topic":"Variable annuity","memory":""},{"front":"Equity-indexed annuity","back":"A fixed annuity whose interest is linked to an index. The participation rate sets how much of index growth is credited.","topic":"Types","memory":""},{"front":"Straight life payout option","back":"Pays the highest monthly income for life, then stops at death with no refund to a beneficiary.","topic":"Payout options","memory":"Straight life pays most, stops at death"},{"front":"Period certain vs refund","back":"Period certain guarantees time (years of payments). Refund guarantees money (return of the purchase amount). Refund options lower monthly payments.","topic":"Payout options","memory":"Period = time; Refund = money"},{"front":"Joint and survivor annuity","back":"Pays over two lives and continues to the survivor, possibly at a reduced percentage such as 100%, 66.7% or 50%. Payments are lower than single life.","topic":"Payout options","memory":""},{"front":"Early withdrawal costs","back":"May trigger an insurer surrender charge and a 10% federal tax penalty on earnings withdrawn before age 59 1/2, in addition to income tax.","topic":"Charges and tax","memory":"Two hits: surrender charge AND 10% penalty"},{"front":"Annuity withdrawal tax order (LIFO)","back":"Earnings are generally withdrawn first and taxed as ordinary income. Contracts issued before August 14, 1982 use FIFO, with basis out first.","topic":"Taxation","memory":""},{"front":"403(b) / TSA","back":"A tax-sheltered annuity plan for employees of public schools and 501(c)(3) nonprofit organizations.","topic":"Qualified plans","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"In a variable annuity, who bears the investment risk?","choices":["The insurer","The owner","The state","The agent"],"answer":1,"explain":"Variable annuity values depend on separate account performance, so the owner bears the risk. In a fixed annuity the insurer does.","topic":"Fixed vs variable"},{"q":"Which annuity payout option pays the highest monthly income with no refund at death?","choices":["Life with period certain","Joint and survivor","Straight life","Cash refund"],"answer":2,"explain":"Straight life has no guarantee for beneficiaries, so it pays the most.","topic":"Payout options"},{"q":"What is the annuitant?","choices":["The insurance company","The person whose life determines the length of payments","The owner's attorney","Any beneficiary"],"answer":1,"explain":"The annuitant's life sets the payout length. Owner and annuitant may be different people.","topic":"Parties"},{"q":"What does the accumulation phase of an annuity do?","choices":["Pays out income","Builds value tax-deferred","Transfers ownership","Pays a death benefit equal to a stated face amount"],"answer":1,"explain":"Money grows tax-deferred during accumulation. Payouts begin in the annuity phase.","topic":"Accumulation"},{"q":"The refund options on an annuity payout generally cause monthly payments to be:","choices":["Higher than straight life","Lower than straight life","Identical to straight life","Tax-free"],"answer":1,"explain":"A refund guarantee costs the insurer more, so monthly income is reduced compared with straight life.","topic":"Payout options"},{"q":"At the latest, a single premium immediate annuity must start paying within:","choices":["30 days","60 days","12 months","5 years"],"answer":2,"explain":"An SPIA must begin within 12 months, though payments often start sooner, within about 30 days.","topic":"Timing"},{"q":"A section 1035 exchange is NOT permitted from:","choices":["Annuity to annuity","Life insurance to annuity","Endowment to annuity","Annuity to life insurance"],"answer":3,"explain":"A tax-free exchange can go from life insurance or an annuity into an annuity, but not from an annuity into life insurance.","topic":"1035 exchange"},{"q":"Which tax rate or penalty applies to early withdrawals from an annuity before age 59 1/2 (earnings, absent an exception)?","choices":["5%","10%","25%","50%"],"answer":1,"explain":"A 10% federal penalty applies, in addition to ordinary income tax and any surrender charge.","topic":"Charges and tax"},{"q":"Which statement about 403(b) plans is NOT correct?","choices":["They are for public school employees","They are for 501(c)(3) nonprofit employees","They are for all private for-profit employees","They are also known as TSAs"],"answer":2,"explain":"403(b) plans cover public school employees and 501(c)(3) nonprofit employees, not all private employees.","topic":"Qualified plans"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of a variable annuity?","choices":["The owner chooses among subaccounts","A prospectus is required","The insurer guarantees the investment return","A securities license is required to sell it"],"answer":2,"explain":"The investment return is not guaranteed; the owner bears the risk.","topic":"Fixed vs variable"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT true once an annuity has been annuitized?","choices":["A payout option has been selected","Payments follow the chosen option","The annuitant's life or period sets the payments","The contract can still be surrendered for cash"],"answer":3,"explain":"Once annuitized, the contract can no longer be surrendered.","topic":"Payout options"},{"q":"When a corporation owns an annuity and the annuitant is not a natural person, what is generally the result?","choices":["The income is tax-deferred","Interest is taxed yearly as ordinary income","The 10% penalty is waived","The annuity is tax-free"],"answer":1,"explain":"Non-natural owners lose tax deferral, except in listed exceptions such as a trust or agent, estate, qualified plan or an immediate annuity.","topic":"Corporate-owned"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Lena, age 66, wants a guaranteed income she cannot outlive and does not want to take market risk. She is not concerned with leaving money to heirs.","q":"Which choice best matches her needs?","choices":["A variable annuity with growth subaccounts only","A fixed annuity paying straight life income","A term policy","An annuity with a period certain only of 5 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fixed means the insurer bears risk, and straight life gives the highest lifetime income with no refund.","topic":"Suitability"},{"situation":"Ben owns a variable annuity during the accumulation phase and wants to move the money to another annuity without paying tax now. He asks about a replacement.","q":"What applies?","choices":["He must surrender it and pay tax","A 1035 exchange can defer the tax, and the basis carries over","The exchange permanently eliminates the tax","He may only exchange into a life policy"],"answer":1,"explain":"A 1035 exchange defers tax, does not eliminate it, and the basis carries over.","topic":"1035 exchange"},{"situation":"A retired couple buys a joint and survivor annuity. After the first spouse dies, the contract pays the survivor.","q":"Which statement is correct?","choices":["Payments stop at the first death","Payments continue to the survivor, possibly at a reduced percentage depending on the contract","The survivor receives a refund of the principal only","The payments become taxable at 10%"],"answer":1,"explain":"A joint and survivor option continues to the surviving annuitant, at 100% or a reduced percentage per the contract.","topic":"Payout options"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Annuitant","match":"Person whose life determines annuity payment length"},{"term":"Straight life","match":"Highest lifetime income, no refund at death"},{"term":"Period certain","match":"Guarantees a set time of payments"},{"term":"Fixed annuity","match":"General account; insurer bears investment risk"},{"term":"Variable annuity","match":"Separate account; owner bears investment risk"},{"term":"403(b) plan","match":"Tax-sheltered annuity for schools and 501(c)(3) nonprofits"}]},"ch10":{"title":"Chapter 10 - Social Security","flashcards":[{"front":"How many quarters make a person fully insured?","back":"40 quarters (about 10 years). They need not be consecutive, and you can earn at most 4 per year.","topic":"Insured status","memory":"FULLY = full retirement and disability."},{"front":"What is currently insured status?","back":"6 of the last 13 quarters (about 3 years). It qualifies the family for survivor benefits only.","topic":"Insured status","memory":"CURRENTLY = only the family gets death benefits."},{"front":"AIME vs. PIA?","back":"AIME is average indexed monthly earnings (the input). PIA is the full benefit at full retirement age (the output).","topic":"Benefit math","memory":"AIME = input, PIA = output."},{"front":"FRA for anyone born in 1960 or later?","back":"Age 67. Before 1938 it was 65, and it rises gradually for births 1938 to 1959.","topic":"Retirement","memory":""},{"front":"Benefit if claimed at 62 with FRA 67?","back":"About 70% of PIA, permanently reduced. The 80% figure applies only to FRA 65.","topic":"Retirement","memory":""},{"front":"2026 earnings limit for someone under FRA all year?","back":"$24,480 a year. $1 is withheld for every $2 earned above the limit.","topic":"Earnings test","memory":"Under FRA: $1 for $2."},{"front":"2026 earnings test in the year you reach FRA?","back":"$65,160 limit, $1 withheld per $3 over it, counting only months before the FRA month. No limit from the FRA month on.","topic":"Earnings test","memory":"FRA year: $1 for $3."},{"front":"Lump-sum death benefit amount?","back":"$255, unchanged for decades. Paid to an eligible spouse or, in some cases, children.","topic":"Survivor benefits","memory":""},{"front":"When does the blackout period start and end?","back":"It starts when the youngest child turns 16 (or at once if no children) and ends when the spouse reaches 60. Child benefits run to 18.","topic":"Blackout","memory":"Blackout starts at 16, not 18."},{"front":"Survivor benefit percentages for parents?","back":"One dependent parent gets 82.5% of PIA. If two parents qualify, each gets 75%. A child gets 75%.","topic":"Survivor benefits","memory":"One parent 82.5, two parents 75 each."},{"front":"What is the dual benefit rule?","back":"A person eligible for two benefits collects only one, the higher amount, not both.","topic":"Dual benefit","memory":""},{"front":"SSDI vs. SSI?","back":"SSDI is based on work credits. SSI is based on financial need and needs no work history, but disability must still be proven.","topic":"Disability","memory":""},{"front":"SSDI work requirement and waiting period?","back":"Fully insured, with 20 of the last 40 quarters (age 31+). 5-month waiting period; benefits begin in the 6th month.","topic":"Disability","memory":""},{"front":"What happens to SSDI at FRA?","back":"Disability benefits convert to retirement benefits at FRA (66 or 67 for most people now), not at 65.","topic":"Disability","memory":""},{"front":"Which Medicare parts are optional, and who runs Medicare?","back":"Parts B, C and D are optional; Part A is mandatory and payroll-tax funded. CMS administers Medicare, and the Part B premium is set each year by CMS.","topic":"Medicare","memory":"A = hospital, B = medical."}],"questions":[{"q":"Which term means the full Social Security benefit payable at full retirement age?","choices":["AIME","PIA","COLA","SGA"],"answer":1,"explain":"PIA is the primary insurance amount, the benefit at FRA. AIME is only the earnings average used to compute it.","topic":"Benefit math"},{"q":"A person who is currently insured but not fully insured can receive which benefits?","choices":["Retirement only","Retirement and disability","Survivor benefits only","No benefits"],"answer":2,"explain":"Currently insured status (6 of the last 13 quarters) supports survivor benefits only. Retirement and disability need fully insured status.","topic":"Insured status"},{"q":"Social Security is best described as which type of program?","choices":["A welfare program","A means-tested program","An entitlement social insurance program","A private savings plan"],"answer":2,"explain":"You pay FICA taxes in and receive benefits later. It is not welfare, and it does not depend on need (that describes SSI).","topic":"Basics"},{"q":"What does SGA stand for in the SSA definition of disability?","choices":["Supplemental guaranteed annuity","Substantial gainful activity","Standard group allowance","Survivor gross allotment"],"answer":1,"explain":"A person is totally disabled if unable to perform substantial gainful activity in any job in the national economy.","topic":"Disability"},{"q":"How many quarters are needed to be fully insured?","choices":["20","30","40","52"],"answer":2,"explain":"40 quarters, about 10 years. Max 4 per year, and they need not be consecutive.","topic":"Insured status"},{"q":"What is the 2026 Social Security taxable maximum?","choices":["$160,200","$176,100","$184,500","No maximum"],"answer":2,"explain":"The 2026 taxable maximum is $184,500. Only Medicare tax has no maximum, which is the tempting distractor.","topic":"FICA"},{"q":"How long is the SSDI waiting period?","choices":["5 months","6 months","12 months","24 months"],"answer":0,"explain":"There is a 5-month wait, so benefits begin in the 6th month. The 12-month figure is how long the disability must last.","topic":"Disability"},{"q":"A worker born in 1962 claims at 62. About what share of PIA is paid?","choices":["100%","80%","75%","70%"],"answer":3,"explain":"With FRA 67, claiming at 62 pays about 70% of PIA. The 80% figure applies only to FRA 65.","topic":"Retirement"},{"q":"Which statement about the blackout period is NOT correct?","choices":["It ends when the surviving spouse reaches 60","Children's benefits continue during it","It starts when the youngest child turns 18","It starts immediately if there are no children"],"answer":2,"explain":"It starts when the youngest child turns 16, not 18. Child benefits run to 18 (19 if in high school).","topic":"Blackout"},{"q":"Which statement about SSDI is NOT correct?","choices":["It converts to retirement benefits at FRA","There is no partial disability benefit","It requires the disability to last at least 12 months or result in death","It ends automatically at age 65 for everyone"],"answer":3,"explain":"SSDI converts to retirement at FRA, which is 66 or 67 for most people now, not at 65.","topic":"Disability"},{"q":"Which statement about Medicare is NOT correct?","choices":["It is administered by CMS","Part B is optional and requires a premium","Part C is Medicare Advantage","It is administered by the OASDHI program"],"answer":3,"explain":"CMS administers Medicare. The OASDHI program is not the administrator.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"Which statement about survivor benefit amounts is NOT correct?","choices":["A child receives 75% of PIA","One dependent parent receives 82.5% of PIA","Each of two dependent parents receives 75% of PIA","Each of two dependent parents receives 82.5% of PIA"],"answer":3,"explain":"82.5% goes to ONE dependent parent. When two parents qualify, each gets 75%.","topic":"Survivor benefits"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Priya is 40 and has earned 30 quarters of coverage. All of her work was in the last few years, and she has more than 6 quarters in the last 13.","q":"What is her insured status?","choices":["Fully insured only","Neither fully nor currently insured","Both fully and currently insured","Currently insured but not fully insured"],"answer":3,"explain":"30 quarters is fewer than the 40 needed to be fully insured. Having 6 or more of the last 13 quarters makes her currently insured, which covers survivor benefits only.","topic":"Insured status"},{"situation":"A 45-year-old widow does not work. Her deceased husband was insured, and their child is 15.","q":"Is she receiving survivor benefits now, and when will the blackout end?","choices":["Yes now; the blackout starts at the child's 16th birthday and ends when she turns 60","No; the blackout is already running and ends at 62","No; benefits start only when she turns 60","Yes now; the blackout starts at the child's 18th birthday and ends at 65"],"answer":0,"explain":"A spouse caring for a child under 16 is paid. The blackout begins when the youngest child turns 16 and ends when she reaches 60.","topic":"Blackout"},{"situation":"Tom is 64 in 2026 and has FRA 67. He collects retirement benefits while earning $30,000 from a part-time job.","q":"How much is withheld for the year under the 2026 earnings test?","choices":["$0, because there is no limit at his age","$2,760","$5,520","$1,840"],"answer":1,"explain":"He is under FRA all year, so the limit is $24,480. Excess is $5,520, and $1 per $2 gives $2,760. $5,520 is the excess before applying the 2-for-1 rule.","topic":"Earnings test"}],"pairs":[{"term":"AIME","match":"Average indexed monthly earnings; input to PIA"},{"term":"PIA","match":"Full benefit payable at full retirement age"},{"term":"SSI","match":"Disability or age benefit based on financial need"},{"term":"Part B","match":"Optional Medicare coverage for outpatient care"},{"term":"Blackout period","match":"Spouse gets nothing from child's 16th birthday to spouse's 60th"},{"term":"Currently insured","match":"6 of last 13 quarters; survivor benefits only"}]},"ch11":{"title":"Chapter 11 - Retirement Plans","flashcards":[{"front":"Qualified vs. nonqualified plan?","back":"Qualified plans follow IRS rules and ERISA, get tax breaks, and cannot favor the highly paid. Nonqualified plans get no tax breaks and can favor executives.","topic":"Plan types","memory":""},{"front":"Defined contribution vs. defined benefit?","back":"DC sets the contribution and the employee bears investment risk. DB sets the benefit by formula and the employer bears the risk.","topic":"Plan types","memory":"DC = Dollars Contributed. DB = Dollars Benefit."},{"front":"Which ERISA exemptions are correct?","back":"Church and governmental plans are exempt. Collectively bargained (union) plans are generally NOT exempt.","topic":"ERISA","memory":""},{"front":"ERISA participation rule and annual form?","back":"Age 21 with 1 year of service (2 years if 100% immediate vesting). Plans file Form 5500 annually; the summary plan description is due within 90 days.","topic":"ERISA","memory":""},{"front":"When is a plan top-heavy?","back":"When more than 60% of assets belong to key employees, measured on the last day of the prior plan year.","topic":"ERISA","memory":""},{"front":"Who can use a 403(b)?","back":"Only 501(c)(3) nonprofits, public school employees, and some ministers. A for-profit employer uses a 401(k).","topic":"Plan types","memory":""},{"front":"2026 401(k) employee deferral limit?","back":"$24,500 for 2026.","topic":"401(k)","memory":""},{"front":"SIMPLE plan key facts?","back":"For employers with 100 or fewer employees, $5,000 prior-year pay, no other qualified plan, and immediate 100% vesting.","topic":"Small business","memory":"SIMPLE = 100 or fewer, instant vesting."},{"front":"What is a SARSEP?","back":"Salary reduction SEP for employers with 25 or fewer employees, set up before 1997. No new SARSEPs; existing ones may continue.","topic":"Small business","memory":"SARSEP = Salary + pre-1997."},{"front":"RMD starting age under SECURE 2.0?","back":"73 for people born 1951 to 1959, and 75 for those born 1960 or later.","topic":"RMDs","memory":""},{"front":"Penalty for missing an RMD now?","back":"25% of the shortfall, or 10% if corrected quickly.","topic":"RMDs","memory":""},{"front":"Inherited IRA rule for most non-spouse beneficiaries?","back":"They must empty the account within 10 years. Narrow exceptions include minor children of the owner, disabled or chronically ill people, and those not over 10 years younger.","topic":"Inherited IRA","memory":""},{"front":"When is a Roth IRA withdrawal tax-free?","back":"The account must be at least 5 years old AND a qualifying event occurs: age 59 1/2, death, disability, or first home purchase ($10,000 lifetime).","topic":"Roth IRA","memory":""},{"front":"Direct vs. indirect rollover?","back":"Direct (trustee to trustee) has no withholding. Indirect has 20% withheld, and the owner must replace it from other money within 60 days.","topic":"Rollovers","memory":"Direct = no withholding."},{"front":"Coverdell ESA vs. 529 plan?","back":"Coverdell: $2,000 per child a year, child under 18. A 529 is a state-operated plan with large limits: college savings or prepaid tuition.","topic":"Education savings","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which type of plan can favor highly paid employees?","choices":["Qualified plan","Nonqualified plan","401(k) plan","ERISA-covered pension"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nonqualified plans such as NQDCs and SERPs can favor executives. Qualified plans cannot discriminate in favor of the highly paid.","topic":"Plan types"},{"q":"Who bears the investment risk in a defined contribution plan?","choices":["The employer","The PBGC","The plan actuary","The employee"],"answer":3,"explain":"In a DC plan the benefit depends on contributions plus investment results, so the employee bears the risk. In a DB plan the employer does.","topic":"Plan types"},{"q":"What does a 457 plan serve?","choices":["Any private employer","State and local government and some nonprofit employees","Only self-employed people","Only public school teachers"],"answer":1,"explain":"457 plans are for state and local government and some nonprofit employees. 403(b) is the one for 501(c)(3)s and public schools.","topic":"Plan types"},{"q":"Which Act introduced the Roth IRA?","choices":["ERISA of 1974","Pension Protection Act of 2006","Taxpayer Relief Act of 1997","SECURE Act of 2019"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Taxpayer Relief Act of 1997 created the Roth IRA. ERISA is the 1974 law protecting retirement benefits.","topic":"Roth IRA"},{"q":"How much is withheld on an indirect rollover from a qualified plan?","choices":["10%","20%","25%","0%"],"answer":1,"explain":"20% is withheld on an indirect rollover. A direct trustee-to-trustee rollover has no withholding.","topic":"Rollovers"},{"q":"How long does the owner have to complete a rollover to avoid tax?","choices":["30 days","45 days","60 days","90 days"],"answer":2,"explain":"The 60-day rule applies. The 90-day figure is the deadline for the summary plan description.","topic":"Rollovers"},{"q":"What is the early withdrawal penalty on a traditional IRA before age 59 1/2?","choices":["5%","10%","20%","25%"],"answer":1,"explain":"10% plus income tax. The 25% figure is the current RMD-miss penalty, a different rule.","topic":"Traditional IRA"},{"q":"What is the lifetime limit for the first-home-purchase exception (IRA penalty and Roth qualified withdrawals)?","choices":["$5,000","$10,000","$15,000","$24,500"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first-time home purchase exception is $10,000 lifetime, for both traditional IRA penalty relief and Roth qualified withdrawals.","topic":"Early withdrawals"},{"q":"Which statement about ERISA is NOT correct?","choices":["It requires annual Form 5500 reporting","Church plans are exempt","Collectively bargained plans are generally exempt","Employee contributions are always 100% vested"],"answer":2,"explain":"Union plans are generally covered by ERISA. Only church and governmental plans are exempt.","topic":"ERISA"},{"q":"Which statement about a Roth IRA is NOT correct?","choices":["Contributions are made with after-tax dollars","There are no RMDs during the owner's life","Contributions are tax-deductible","Contributions can always be withdrawn tax-free"],"answer":2,"explain":"Roth contributions are not deductible. Their payoff is tax-free qualified withdrawals, and contributions come out tax-free because they were already taxed.","topic":"Roth IRA"},{"q":"Which statement about RMDs is NOT correct?","choices":["The age is 73 for people born 1951 to 1959","The age is 75 for people born 1960 or later","The penalty for a missed RMD is 50%","Roth IRAs have no RMDs during the owner's life"],"answer":2,"explain":"SECURE 2.0 set the penalty at 25% (10% if corrected quickly), not 50%.","topic":"RMDs"},{"q":"Which statement about inherited IRAs is NOT correct?","choices":["Most non-spouse beneficiaries must empty the account within 10 years","A surviving spouse can roll the account into their own IRA","Most non-spouse beneficiaries can stretch payments over their lifetime","A minor child of the owner is a narrow exception"],"answer":2,"explain":"After the SECURE Act, most non-spouse beneficiaries face the 10-year rule, not a lifetime stretch.","topic":"Inherited IRA"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Lee leaves his job and receives a check payable to himself for his 401(k) balance. The plan withheld 20% for taxes. He deposits the check into an IRA 30 days later.","q":"What should Lee have done to avoid the problem?","choices":["Wait 90 days before depositing","Deposit it into a Roth IRA instead","Cash the check and pay the 10% penalty","Request a direct trustee-to-trustee rollover"],"answer":3,"explain":"A direct rollover has no withholding. Lee must now replace the withheld 20% from other money within 60 days, or that part is taxable and may carry a 10% penalty.","topic":"Rollovers"},{"situation":"A 45-year-old woman works for a for-profit manufacturing company. She asks whether she can contribute to a 403(b) plan through her employer.","q":"What is the answer?","choices":["Yes, any employer can offer a 403(b)","No, 403(b) plans are only for 501(c)(3) nonprofits, public schools and some ministers","Yes, but only after age 50","No, because 403(b) plans are nonqualified"],"answer":1,"explain":"A for-profit employer would use a 401(k) or similar plan. 403(b) is restricted to nonprofits, public schools and some ministers.","topic":"Plan types"},{"situation":"A self-employed consultant has no employees. She wants a retirement plan with high contribution limits and simple administration.","q":"Which plan fits best?","choices":["SARSEP","A SEP (or solo 401(k)-type plan)","A coverdell ESA","A nonqualified deferred compensation plan"],"answer":1,"explain":"A SEP is simple and has much higher limits than an IRA. A SARSEP could not be set up after 1996, and a Coverdell is for education.","topic":"Small business"}],"pairs":[{"term":"ERISA","match":"1974 law protecting retirement benefits; requires Form 5500"},{"term":"NQDC","match":"Nonqualified deferred pay taxed only when paid out"},{"term":"Money purchase plan","match":"Defined contribution plan with a fixed contribution formula"},{"term":"ESOP","match":"Plan where employees own company stock held in trust"},{"term":"Conduit IRA","match":"Temporary holding account for funds moving between qualified plans"},{"term":"Coverdell ESA","match":"Education account: $2,000 per child, child under 18"}]},"ch12":{"title":"Chapter 12 - Health and Accident Insurance","flashcards":[{"front":"Renewability order, least to most favorable to the insured?","back":"Cancellable, optionally renewable, conditionally renewable, guaranteed renewable, noncancellable. The more favorable, the higher the cost.","topic":"Renewability","memory":"CAN, OPTIONAL, CONDITIONAL, GUARANTEED, NON-CANCELLABLE."},{"front":"Most common renewability type for disability income?","back":"Noncancellable: the insurer cannot cancel or change terms, and the premium stays the same.","topic":"Renewability","memory":""},{"front":"Guaranteed renewable vs. noncancellable?","back":"Guaranteed renewable: must renew to a set age, but premium can rise for the whole class. Noncancellable: terms and premium locked in.","topic":"Renewability","memory":""},{"front":"COBRA coverage length by qualifying event?","back":"18 months for job loss or reduced hours (29 if disabled). 36 months for death, divorce, child aging out, or Medicare eligibility.","topic":"COBRA","memory":"18 for the employee leaving, 36 for the family."},{"front":"COBRA employer size, election time, and cost cap?","back":"Employers with 20 or more employees. 60 days to elect. Up to 102% of the premium.","topic":"COBRA","memory":""},{"front":"What did the ACA do to preexisting-condition exclusions?","back":"It banned them in most individual and group plans (2014). The old HIPAA lookback and 63-day break rules are history only.","topic":"HIPAA and ACA","memory":""},{"front":"ACA rules on dependents and benefit limits?","back":"Children may stay on a parent's plan to age 26, married or not. Ten essential health benefits have no lifetime or annual dollar limits.","topic":"HIPAA and ACA","memory":""},{"front":"ACA metal tiers: what does each plan pay?","back":"Bronze 60%, Silver 70%, Gold 80%, Platinum 90% of costs on average.","topic":"HIPAA and ACA","memory":"60-70-80-90."},{"front":"2026 HSA contribution limits and catch-up?","back":"$4,400 self-only, $8,750 family. Catch-up of $1,000 starts at age 55 with no upper age limit. Requires an HDHP.","topic":"HSA","memory":""},{"front":"HSA penalty for nonqualified withdrawals?","back":"20% penalty plus income tax. Qualified medical withdrawals are tax-free. Medigap premiums are NOT qualified.","topic":"HSA","memory":""},{"front":"Who is primary when a worker with a group plan is 65+?","back":"If the employer has 20 or more employees, the group plan is primary and Medicare secondary. Under 20, Medicare is primary.","topic":"Coordination of benefits","memory":""},{"front":"Birthday rule for dependent children?","back":"The plan of the parent whose birthday falls earlier in the calendar year is primary. Each spouse's own plan is primary for that spouse.","topic":"Coordination of benefits","memory":""},{"front":"Accidental means vs. accidental results?","back":"Means: both cause and result must be unintended (stricter). Results: only the result must be unintended. Most states require results.","topic":"AD&D","memory":""},{"front":"Principal sum vs. capital sum in AD&D?","back":"Principal sum is paid for accidental death (100%). Capital sum is a percentage of it for dismemberment. Death must occur within 90 days.","topic":"AD&D","memory":""},{"front":"Tax rule for disability income benefits?","back":"Benefits are tax-free if the insured paid the premiums (individual policy or employee-paid group). Employer-paid group disability benefits are taxable.","topic":"Taxation","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which renewability provision is MOST favorable to the insured?","choices":["Conditionally renewable","Guaranteed renewable","Optionally renewable","Noncancellable"],"answer":3,"explain":"Noncancellable locks in both terms and premium. Guaranteed renewable still lets the insurer raise premiums for the class.","topic":"Renewability"},{"q":"What does the out-of-pocket maximum NOT count?","choices":["Deductibles","Coinsurance","Premiums","Copayments"],"answer":2,"explain":"Premiums and non-covered services do not count toward the out-of-pocket maximum. After it is reached, insurance pays 100% of covered costs.","topic":"Cost sharing"},{"q":"In group insurance, the document given to each employee is called a:","choices":["Master policy","Certificate of insurance","Binder","Rider"],"answer":1,"explain":"The master policy goes to the employer. The certificate goes to each employee and is not a separate policy.","topic":"Group health"},{"q":"What is a 501(c)(9) trust (VEBA) used for?","choices":["Funding employee benefits","Funding retirement plans for the self-employed","Holding HSA funds","Paying workers' compensation claims"],"answer":0,"explain":"A VEBA is a funding vehicle for employee benefits, with deductible contributions and a single purpose.","topic":"Funding"},{"q":"How many days does a person have to elect COBRA coverage?","choices":["30 days","60 days","90 days","102 days"],"answer":1,"explain":"60 days. The 102% figure is the maximum premium share, not a time limit.","topic":"COBRA"},{"q":"After an accident, within how many days must death occur for the AD&D death benefit to apply?","choices":["30 days","60 days","180 days","90 days"],"answer":3,"explain":"The standard time limit is 90 days from the accident.","topic":"AD&D"},{"q":"Under the ACA, how long can a child stay on a parent's health plan?","choices":["Until age 19","Until age 23","Until age 26","Until age 30"],"answer":2,"explain":"Until age 26, whether the child is married or unmarried. Age 30 is the Coverdell rollover age, a different rule.","topic":"ACA"},{"q":"A group has 18 employees and a worker over 65 is covered. Who pays first?","choices":["The group plan","Medicare","They split equally","Neither; a Medigap plan pays first"],"answer":1,"explain":"Employers with fewer than 20 employees: Medicare is primary. With 20 or more, the group plan is primary.","topic":"Coordination of benefits"},{"q":"Which statement about COBRA is NOT correct?","choices":["It applies to employers with 20 or more employees","A covered employee who dies gives dependents 36 months","The participant may be charged up to 102% of the premium","It guarantees coverage at the employer's cost"],"answer":3,"explain":"COBRA continuation is at the participant's own expense. Charging up to 102% covers administration.","topic":"COBRA"},{"q":"Which statement about HIPAA and the ACA is NOT correct?","choices":["HIPAA does not apply to disability income plans","The ACA banned preexisting-condition exclusions in most plans","The old 63-day break rule is still a current rule","HIPAA has Privacy and Security Rules"],"answer":2,"explain":"The 63-day break and creditable coverage rules are old HIPAA rules made obsolete by the ACA. Know them as history.","topic":"HIPAA and ACA"},{"q":"Which statement about HSAs is NOT correct?","choices":["Contributions are limited to 100% of the deductible","An HDHP is required","Earnings grow tax-free","The balance is fully portable"],"answer":0,"explain":"HSA contributions are limited by the annual IRS limit ($4,400 self-only / $8,750 family for 2026), not by the deductible amount.","topic":"HSA"},{"q":"Which is NOT a feature of workers' compensation?","choices":["Employer pays the premiums","Covers job-related injuries only","Employees usually give up the right to sue the employer","Employees pay a deductible and copayment"],"answer":3,"explain":"Workers' compensation typically has no deductibles or copayments, and the employer pays.","topic":"Workers' compensation"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Carla works for an employer with 35 employees. She is terminated for reasons other than gross misconduct and has a family on her group health plan. She is not disabled.","q":"How long can she keep group coverage, and at what maximum cost?","choices":["36 months at 100%","18 months at 102%","29 months at 102%","18 months at 150%"],"answer":1,"explain":"Her own job termination gives 18 months. The employer has 20+ employees so COBRA applies, with a 102% cap. 36 months is for family events like death or divorce; 29 months is only if disabled.","topic":"COBRA"},{"situation":"Matt's individual disability income policy cannot be canceled by the insurer, and the premium can never be changed.","q":"What renewability type is it?","choices":["Guaranteed renewable","Conditionally renewable","Noncancellable","Optionally renewable"],"answer":2,"explain":"Noncancellable means no cancellation and no change in terms or premium. Guaranteed renewable lets premiums change for the class.","topic":"Renewability"},{"situation":"A surgeon loses one hand in an accident. Her AD&D policy pays under a capital sum provision.","q":"How is the benefit determined?","choices":["100% of the principal sum, always","As a percentage of the principal sum set by the policy schedule","It is not covered because she survived","A flat $10,000 benefit"],"answer":1,"explain":"A capital sum is a percentage of the principal sum for dismemberment, per the policy's schedule.","topic":"AD&D"}],"pairs":[{"term":"COBRA","match":"Continuation of group coverage at own expense, up to 102%"},{"term":"Coinsurance","match":"Percentage the insured pays after the deductible"},{"term":"Principal sum","match":"AD&D amount paid for accidental death"},{"term":"Probationary period","match":"Waiting period before group coverage for sickness starts"},{"term":"Business overhead expense","match":"Pays an owner's business expenses during disability, not owner pay"},{"term":"Hospital indemnity","match":"Fixed payment per day of hospital stay"}]},"ch13":{"title":"Chapter 13 - Health Insurance Providers (and Government Health Programs)","flashcards":[{"front":"Commercial insurer vs. service provider payment model?","back":"Commercial insurers reimburse (insured pays first, then files a claim). Service providers like Blue Cross/Blue Shield and HMOs are prepaid and pay the provider directly.","topic":"Provider types","memory":""},{"front":"Blue Cross vs. Blue Shield?","back":"Blue Cross covers hospital expenses. Blue Shield covers surgical and medical (doctor) expenses.","topic":"Provider types","memory":"Cross = hospital. Shield = surgery and doctors."},{"front":"HMO key features?","back":"Prepaid, a PCP gatekeeper, referrals required, no out-of-network coverage except emergencies. Lowest cost of the provider types.","topic":"HMO vs. PPO","memory":""},{"front":"PPO key features?","back":"Fee-for-service at discounted rates, no gatekeeper or referrals, out-of-network covered at reduced benefits. Emergencies covered in full.","topic":"HMO vs. PPO","memory":""},{"front":"POS vs. EPO?","back":"POS requires a PCP and referrals but allows out-of-network at higher cost. EPO needs no PCP and covers no out-of-network except emergencies.","topic":"HMO vs. PPO","memory":""},{"front":"What is capitation?","back":"A fixed payment per member assigned to a physician or medical group, regardless of services used.","topic":"HMO","memory":""},{"front":"Three types of case management review?","back":"Prospective (before admission), concurrent (during the stay), retrospective (after treatment, used for trends, not to deny claims).","topic":"Managed care","memory":"PRO = before, CON-current = during, RETRO = after."},{"front":"Medicare Part A vs. Part B funding and cost?","back":"Part A: payroll taxes, usually premium-free, automatic. Part B: general revenue plus premiums, optional. Part B pays 80% after the deductible.","topic":"Medicare","memory":""},{"front":"Who administers Medicare?","back":"CMS administers Medicare. The Social Security Administration handles enrollment and public information.","topic":"Medicare","memory":""},{"front":"Medicare eligibility for disabled people under 65?","back":"24 months of Social Security disability benefits. ESRD and ALS qualify immediately without the wait.","topic":"Medicare","memory":""},{"front":"When is Medicare primary for age-based coverage?","back":"With employer plans under 20 employees, Medicare pays first. At 20 or more, the group plan is primary. Disability-based line is 100 employees.","topic":"Medicare","memory":"Age: 20. Disability: 100."},{"front":"Medicare assignment vs. excess charge?","back":"With assignment the doctor accepts the Medicare-approved amount as full payment. The excess charge is the extra billed by a doctor who does not accept assignment.","topic":"Medicare","memory":""},{"front":"Medicare vs. Medicaid?","back":"Medicare: federal, age 65+ or disabled, work history based. Medicaid: joint federal-state, run by states, low income and limited assets.","topic":"Government programs","memory":""},{"front":"Four workers' compensation disability types?","back":"Total permanent, permanent partial, temporary total, temporary partial.","topic":"Workers' compensation","memory":""},{"front":"TRICARE options and MET vs. MEWA?","back":"TRICARE: Prime, Extra, Standard. A MET pools small employers, often in one industry. A MEWA is for small employers with a common employment bond, often self-insured.","topic":"Other programs","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which provider type uses a primary care physician as a gatekeeper and is prepaid?","choices":["PPO","HMO","Commercial indemnity insurer","EPO"],"answer":1,"explain":"An HMO is prepaid and uses a PCP gatekeeper. A PPO is fee-for-service with no gatekeeper.","topic":"HMO vs. PPO"},{"q":"What type of case management reviews care after treatment, for trends?","choices":["Prospective","Concurrent","Retrospective","Precertification"],"answer":2,"explain":"Retrospective review happens after treatment and is not used to deny claims. Prospective is before admission, concurrent during the stay.","topic":"Managed care"},{"q":"Which Medicare part is funded by payroll taxes and is usually premium-free?","choices":["Part B","Part C","Part D","Part A"],"answer":3,"explain":"Part A (hospital insurance) is funded by payroll and self-employment taxes. Part B uses general revenue plus premiums.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"Medicaid is best described as:","choices":["A federal program for people over 65","A joint federal-state program for low-income people, run by the states","A private insurance plan for veterans","A program funded only by payroll taxes"],"answer":1,"explain":"Medicaid (Title XIX, 1965) is joint federal-state and need-based. Medicare is the federal age/disability program.","topic":"Government programs"},{"q":"How many months of SSDI must a disabled person under 65 receive before Medicare begins (outside ESRD and ALS)?","choices":["5 months","12 months","36 months","24 months"],"answer":3,"explain":"24 months of Social Security disability benefits. The 5-month figure is the SSDI waiting period itself.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"What does Part B pay after the deductible for covered services?","choices":["50%","80%","100%","90%"],"answer":1,"explain":"Part B pays 80% and the enrollee pays 20% coinsurance after the deductible.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"For age-based Medicare, at what employer size does the group plan become primary?","choices":["10 or more","20 or more","50 or more","100 or more"],"answer":1,"explain":"20 or more employees. The 100-employee line applies to disability-based Medicare.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"A TRICARE option that is fee-for-service and formerly known as CHAMPUS is:","choices":["Prime","Extra","Standard","FEHB"],"answer":2,"explain":"TRICARE Standard is fee-for-service, formerly CHAMPUS. Prime uses military treatment facilities first, and Extra uses a preferred network.","topic":"Other programs"},{"q":"Which statement about PPOs is NOT correct?","choices":["They offer discounted fees through a provider network","They are prepaid plans","Referrals are not required","Out-of-network care is covered at reduced benefits"],"answer":1,"explain":"PPOs are fee-for-service, not prepaid. HMOs are the prepaid type.","topic":"HMO vs. PPO"},{"q":"Which statement about Medicare is NOT correct?","choices":["Part A is usually premium-free if eligible","Part B enrollment is optional","It was established in 1965","It is run by the states"],"answer":3,"explain":"Medicare is a federal program run by CMS. Medicaid is the one administered by the states.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"Which is NOT covered by Medicare Parts A and B?","choices":["Private-duty nursing","Inpatient hospital stays","Doctor visits","Preventive screenings"],"answer":0,"explain":"Private-duty nurses and personal attendants are excluded from both parts. The others are covered under A or B.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"Which statement about workers' compensation benefits is NOT correct?","choices":["Medical benefits have no time limit","Survivor benefits cover dependents and burial expenses","Rehabilitation services are included","Employees pay the premiums"],"answer":3,"explain":"Employers fund workers' compensation through state programs, private insurers or self-insurance. Employees do not pay premiums.","topic":"Workers' compensation"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Ruth wants to see specialists without getting a referral first. She does not mind paying more for care outside the network.","q":"Which structure fits her?","choices":["Staff-model HMO","POS plan","PPO","Closed-panel HMO"],"answer":2,"explain":"A PPO needs no PCP or referrals and covers out-of-network care at a reduced benefit. HMO and POS require referrals.","topic":"HMO vs. PPO"},{"situation":"Greg is 67 and works for a company with 15 employees. He is covered by the company's group health plan and is also eligible for Medicare.","q":"Which coverage pays first?","choices":["The group plan","Medicare","Medicaid","They pay equal shares"],"answer":1,"explain":"With fewer than 20 employees, Medicare is primary for age-based coverage and the group plan is secondary. At 20 or more, the group plan would pay first.","topic":"Medicare"},{"situation":"A small employer with 30 employees, in the same industry as other small employers, wants group health coverage at lower cost by pooling risk.","q":"What arrangement could help?","choices":["A multiple employer trust (MET)","A Health Savings Account only","TRICARE","A consumer HMO under FEHB"],"answer":0,"explain":"A MET lets small employers, often in one industry, pool risk through a joinder agreement. A MEWA is the related arrangement for small employers with a common employment bond.","topic":"Other programs"}],"pairs":[{"term":"HMO","match":"Prepaid plan with PCP gatekeeper and referrals"},{"term":"PPO","match":"Fee-for-service network with discounts and no referrals"},{"term":"Capitation","match":"Fixed payment per member assigned to a physician"},{"term":"Medicaid","match":"State-administered program for low-income people"},{"term":"Medicare Part B","match":"Optional medical coverage with a monthly premium"},{"term":"Precertification","match":"Approval needed before elective surgery or admission"}]},"ch14":{"title":"Chapter 14 - Medical Expense Insurance","flashcards":[{"front":"Basic vs. major medical?","back":"Basic: first-dollar, low limits, short period, no coinsurance. Major: after a deductible, high limits, with coinsurance, for catastrophic protection.","topic":"Basic vs. major","memory":""},{"front":"Comprehensive vs. supplementary major medical?","back":"Comprehensive is ONE combined policy that stands alone. Supplementary is TWO policies (basic plus major) and uses a corridor deductible.","topic":"Basic vs. major","memory":""},{"front":"Name the six types of deductibles.","back":"Flat (initial), corridor, integrated, per-cause, all-cause, and family maximum.","topic":"Deductibles","memory":"FLAT = first; CORRIDOR = middle; INTEGRATED = basic counts."},{"front":"Corridor vs. integrated deductible?","back":"Corridor applies after basic coverage is used up, bridging basic to major. Integrated lets basic benefits count toward the major medical deductible.","topic":"Deductibles","memory":""},{"front":"Per-cause vs. all-cause deductible?","back":"Per-cause: a separate deductible for each illness or accident. All-cause: one deductible per benefit period covering all problems.","topic":"Deductibles","memory":""},{"front":"What is the carryover provision?","back":"Expenses from the last 3 months of the year count toward the next year's deductible.","topic":"Deductibles","memory":""},{"front":"What does coinsurance mean, and what share must the insurer pay?","back":"Insurer and insured share costs after the deductible, written insurer/insured (80/20). The insurer must pay at least 50% to qualify as insurance.","topic":"Coinsurance","memory":""},{"front":"What does a stop-loss provision do?","back":"It caps the insured's total out-of-pocket costs. After the limit, the insurer pays 100%.","topic":"Coinsurance","memory":""},{"front":"Order of the claims calculation?","back":"Subtract the deductible first, then apply coinsurance to what remains, then check the stop-loss cap.","topic":"Claims math","memory":"Deductible first, then coinsurance."},{"front":"Three surgical expense payment methods?","back":"Surgical schedule (fixed dollars per procedure), relative value (units times a conversion factor), and UCR (area-based usual, customary, reasonable).","topic":"Surgical expense","memory":""},{"front":"Who receives hospital indemnity benefits, and on what basis?","back":"The insured receives a fixed amount per day, week or month, based on days confined, not on actual expenses.","topic":"Specialty policies","memory":""},{"front":"Critical illness vs. dread disease policy?","back":"Critical illness pays a lump sum on diagnosis if the insured survives a set period. Dread disease pays fixed amounts for named serious diseases.","topic":"Specialty policies","memory":""},{"front":"HSA vs. HRA vs. FSA?","back":"HSA: individual-owned, needs an HDHP, portable. HRA: employer-funded only. FSA: employee-funded through a cafeteria plan, use it or lose it (limited carryover may be allowed).","topic":"Tax-advantaged accounts","memory":"HRA = employer; FSA = use it or lose it."},{"front":"How does intensive care and miscellaneous hospital coverage work?","back":"Intensive care typically pays double the room-and-board benefit. Miscellaneous services are about 15 times room and board.","topic":"Hospital expense","memory":""},{"front":"Are non-prescription drugs covered under drug coverage?","back":"No, except injectable insulin. Devices, needles and administration fees are also not covered, and coverage follows a drug formulary.","topic":"Prescription drugs","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which medical coverage is first-dollar with low limits and no coinsurance?","choices":["Major medical","Comprehensive major medical","Basic medical","Supplementary major medical"],"answer":2,"explain":"Basic medical covers routine care from the first dollar with low limits. Major medical applies a deductible and coinsurance.","topic":"Basic vs. major"},{"q":"Which deductible type is applied AFTER basic coverage is used up?","choices":["Flat","Per-cause","Corridor","Family maximum"],"answer":2,"explain":"A corridor deductible bridges basic coverage to major medical. An integrated deductible, by contrast, lets basic benefits count toward it.","topic":"Deductibles"},{"q":"Which account can be funded ONLY by the employer?","choices":["HSA","HRA","FSA","MSA"],"answer":1,"explain":"An HRA is employer-funded only. HSAs can be funded by individual or employer, and FSAs by the employee.","topic":"Tax-advantaged accounts"},{"q":"A surgical payment based on units multiplied by a conversion factor is called:","choices":["Surgical schedule","UCR","Per-cause","Relative value"],"answer":3,"explain":"Relative value pays units times a factor (20 units x $50 = $1,000). A schedule pays a fixed dollar amount per procedure.","topic":"Surgical expense"},{"q":"A policy has a $750 deductible and 70/30 coinsurance. On $2,100 of expenses, how much does the insurer pay?","choices":["$945","$1,470","$1,155","$1,350"],"answer":0,"explain":"$2,100 minus $750 is $1,350, and 70% of that is $945. $1,155 is what the insured pays.","topic":"Claims math"},{"q":"What is the minimum share the insurer must pay for a coinsurance policy to qualify as insurance?","choices":["25%","40%","50%","70%"],"answer":2,"explain":"At least 50% of the expense. Typical in-network shares of 70% to 90% are common, not the legal minimum.","topic":"Coinsurance"},{"q":"Intensive care in a hospital expense policy typically pays:","choices":["Half the room-and-board benefit","The same as room and board","Double the room-and-board benefit","15 times room and board"],"answer":2,"explain":"ICU pays double room and board. The 15 times figure is for miscellaneous hospital services.","topic":"Hospital expense"},{"q":"How much of the expenses count toward a deductible under the carryover provision?","choices":["Expenses from the last 3 months of the year","Expenses from the first 3 months of the year","All expenses from the prior year","Only prescription costs"],"answer":0,"explain":"The last 3 months of the year carry over to count toward the NEXT year's deductible.","topic":"Deductibles"},{"q":"Which statement about hospital indemnity policies is NOT correct?","choices":["Benefits are paid to the insured","Benefits are a fixed amount per day, week or month","Benefits depend on days confined","Benefits are based on actual medical expenses"],"answer":3,"explain":"Hospital indemnity pays a fixed benefit regardless of actual bills. Reimbursement policies pay actual charges.","topic":"Specialty policies"},{"q":"Which statement about prescription drug coverage is NOT correct?","choices":["It follows a drug formulary","Injectable insulin is an exception to the non-prescription exclusion","All non-prescription drugs are covered","Devices and needles are usually not covered"],"answer":2,"explain":"Non-prescription drugs are not covered except injectable insulin.","topic":"Prescription drugs"},{"q":"Which statement about FSAs is NOT correct?","choices":["They are funded by the employee through a cafeteria plan","They require a high-deductible health plan","They are generally use it or lose it","Some employers allow a limited carryover or grace period"],"answer":1,"explain":"An HDHP is required for an HSA, not an FSA.","topic":"Tax-advantaged accounts"},{"q":"Which statement about comprehensive major medical is NOT correct?","choices":["It is a single combined policy","It uses a standard deductible","It covers nearly all expenses","It requires a separate basic policy first"],"answer":3,"explain":"A separate basic policy is a feature of SUPPLEMENTARY major medical, which uses two policies.","topic":"Basic vs. major"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Ben has $5,000 of covered medical bills. His policy has a $1,000 deductible and 80/20 coinsurance, with no stop-loss reached.","q":"How much does Ben pay in total?","choices":["$800","$1,000","$1,800","$4,000"],"answer":2,"explain":"$5,000 minus $1,000 is $4,000. The insurer pays 80% ($3,200), and Ben pays the $1,000 deductible plus 20% ($800), a total of $1,800.","topic":"Claims math"},{"situation":"Lisa wants a catastrophic-cost policy that is separate from her existing basic plan and sits on top of it once basic benefits are used up.","q":"Which type of coverage is this?","choices":["Comprehensive major medical","Hospital indemnity","Supplementary major medical","Dread disease policy"],"answer":2,"explain":"Supplementary major medical is a second policy that supplements basic benefits, typically with a corridor deductible. Comprehensive is one policy.","topic":"Basic vs. major"},{"situation":"A worker wants a health account that she personally owns, that is portable and works together with a high-deductible health plan.","q":"Which account fits?","choices":["HRA","FSA","HSA","Employer-only reimbursement account"],"answer":2,"explain":"An HSA is individual-owned, requires an HDHP, and is portable. An HRA is employer-funded and owned, and an FSA is use it or lose it.","topic":"Tax-advantaged accounts"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Corridor deductible","match":"Applies after basic coverage is exhausted, bridging to major medical"},{"term":"Integrated deductible","match":"Basic benefits count toward the major medical deductible"},{"term":"Stop-loss","match":"Caps insured's out-of-pocket costs; insurer then pays 100%"},{"term":"Relative value","match":"Surgical payment: units times a conversion factor"},{"term":"HRA","match":"Reimbursement account funded only by the employer"},{"term":"Critical illness","match":"Lump sum on diagnosis if insured survives a set period"}]},"ch15":{"title":"Chapter 15 - Disability Income Insurance","flashcards":[{"front":"Own-occupation definition","back":"Pays if the insured cannot perform the duties of THEIR OWN job. Broader coverage for the insured, so it costs more.","topic":"Own vs any occupation","memory":"OWN = Only my job."},{"front":"Any-occupation definition","back":"Pays only if the insured cannot do ANY job reasonably suited by education, training, or experience. Cheaper.","topic":"Own vs any occupation","memory":"ANY = any job at all."},{"front":"Presumptive total disability conditions","back":"Total blindness, total deafness, loss of speech, loss of two or more limbs. Pays the full benefit automatically.","topic":"Total disability","memory":""},{"front":"Typical partial disability benefit","back":"About 50% of the monthly benefit for 3 to 6 months, generally after a period of total disability.","topic":"Partial disability","memory":""},{"front":"Flat vs residual partial benefit","back":"Flat: fixed amount (usually 50% of full benefit). Residual: percent of income lost times the monthly benefit.","topic":"Partial disability","memory":""},{"front":"Accidental means vs accidental results","back":"Means: both cause AND result must be accidental (strict). Results: only the result must be unexpected (standard today).","topic":"Accidental means","memory":""},{"front":"Probationary period","back":"Starts at policy issue, 15, 30, or 60 days, applies to sickness only (not accidents), one time only.","topic":"Waiting periods","memory":"Probationary = start of the POLICY."},{"front":"Elimination period","back":"Waiting time after each disability begins, commonly 30 to 90 days. Longer elimination period = lower premium.","topic":"Waiting periods","memory":"Elimination = start of each DISABILITY."},{"front":"Benefit period length and premium","back":"Maximum time benefits are paid. Short-term is 6 months to 2 years; long-term is over 2 years. Longer = higher premium.","topic":"Benefit period","memory":""},{"front":"Recurrent disability rule","back":"Same disability returning within the set period (typically 90 days or 6 months) is a continuation with no new elimination period.","topic":"Recurrent disability","memory":""},{"front":"Loss of earning test","back":"Counts active income only (wages, commissions, fees), not passive income. Income must drop by at least 20% to qualify.","topic":"Benefit calculation","memory":""},{"front":"Waiver of premium rider","back":"Waives premiums during total disability, usually after 3 or 6 months, ending around age 60 or 65. Not a loan and pays no income.","topic":"Riders","memory":""},{"front":"Hospital confinement rider","back":"Waives the elimination period if the insured is hospitalized. Benefits start on day one, usually for 6 to 12 months.","topic":"Riders","memory":""},{"front":"Business overhead expense (BOE) coverage","back":"Pays the business's operating expenses (rent, utilities, employee salaries) during the owner's disability. Does NOT pay the owner's salary.","topic":"Business uses","memory":""},{"front":"Disability buy-sell elimination period","back":"Often up to 2 years, to make sure the disability is permanent before the interest is bought out.","topic":"Business uses","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which coverage pays if the insured cannot perform the duties of his or her own job, even if another job is possible?","choices":["Any-occupation","Own-occupation","Residual","Presumptive"],"answer":1,"explain":"Own-occupation looks only at the insured's own job. Any-occupation is the tempting one, but it pays only if no suitable job can be done.","topic":"Own vs any occupation"},{"q":"What is a probationary period in a disability income policy?","choices":["A wait after each disability begins","The maximum time benefits are paid","A one-time wait after policy issue that applies to sickness only","A period when premiums are waived"],"answer":2,"explain":"Probationary runs from the issue date, once, for sickness only. The elimination period is the wait after each disability begins.","topic":"Waiting periods"},{"q":"Which of these is a presumptive total disability?","choices":["Loss of two or more limbs","A broken arm","Loss of one finger","Temporary hearing loss"],"answer":0,"explain":"Presumptive disabilities are total blindness, total deafness, loss of speech, and loss of two or more limbs. Minor or temporary losses do not qualify automatically.","topic":"Total disability"},{"q":"Under accidental results, what must be unexpected?","choices":["Only the cause","Both the cause and the result","The insured's activity","Only the result"],"answer":3,"explain":"Accidental results requires only that the RESULT be unexpected. Requiring both cause and result is the stricter accidental means rule.","topic":"Accidental means"},{"q":"By how much must income drop to qualify under the loss of earning test?","choices":["10%","20%","50%","60%"],"answer":1,"explain":"The test requires at least a 20% drop in active income. 60% is the usual percent-of-earnings benefit, not the qualifying drop.","topic":"Benefit calculation"},{"q":"Under the percent-of-earnings method, what percentage of pre-disability earnings is usually paid?","choices":["60%","50%","20%","100%"],"answer":0,"explain":"The usual figure is 60%, so $5,000 a month of earnings gives $3,000. 50% is the typical flat partial disability benefit.","topic":"Benefit calculation"},{"q":"A typical partial disability benefit is what share of the monthly benefit, and for how long?","choices":["100% for 12 months","60% for 2 years","50% for 3 to 6 months","20% for 90 days"],"answer":2,"explain":"Partial disability usually pays about 50% for 3 to 6 months to encourage a return to work.","topic":"Partial disability"},{"q":"The elimination period in a disability buy-sell agreement is often up to:","choices":["30 days","90 days","6 months","2 years"],"answer":3,"explain":"A long wait (up to 2 years) confirms the disability is permanent. 30 to 90 days is the typical key person elimination period.","topic":"Business uses"},{"q":"Which statement about the probationary period is NOT true?","choices":["It applies to sickness only","It happens after the policy issue date","It occurs one time only","It applies to every disability claim"],"answer":3,"explain":"Applying to every claim describes the elimination period. The probationary period is one time only.","topic":"Waiting periods"},{"q":"Which expense does business overhead expense insurance NOT pay?","choices":["Rent","Employee salaries","The disabled owner's salary","Equipment leases"],"answer":2,"explain":"BOE pays operating expenses of the business. The owner's own salary is not covered.","topic":"Business uses"},{"q":"Which statement about the waiver of premium rider is NOT correct?","choices":["It pays the insured a monthly income","It keeps the policy in force during total disability","It usually ends at age 60 or 65","It is not a loan"],"answer":0,"explain":"Waiver of premium waives premiums only; it pays no income. Premiums are waived, not deferred or borrowed.","topic":"Riders"},{"q":"Which is NOT a common exclusion in disability income policies?","choices":["Attempted suicide","War and military service","Aviation (pilots and crew)","Sickness requiring hospitalization"],"answer":3,"explain":"Disability policies exist to cover sickness. Common exclusions are war, attempted suicide, overseas residence, aviation, and felonies.","topic":"Exclusions"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"A dentist loses the use of her hands and can no longer practice dentistry. She can still teach dental students.","q":"Does she receive benefits under an own-occupation policy and under an any-occupation policy?","choices":["Both policies pay","Neither policy pays","Own-occupation pays; any-occupation does not","Any-occupation pays; own-occupation does not"],"answer":2,"explain":"She cannot do her own job, so own-occupation pays. She can still teach, a suitable job, so any-occupation does not.","topic":"Own vs any occupation"},{"situation":"Paul buys a disability policy with a 90-day elimination period instead of a 30-day one.","q":"What happens to his premium?","choices":["It goes down","It goes up","It stays the same","It is waived"],"answer":0,"explain":"A longer elimination period works like a bigger time deductible, so the premium is lower.","topic":"Waiting periods"},{"situation":"A business owner wants to keep the business running and cover rent and employee salaries while he is disabled.","q":"Which coverage fits?","choices":["Disability buy-sell","Key person disability","Business overhead expense","Reducing term"],"answer":2,"explain":"BOE pays ongoing operating expenses. It does not pay the owner's salary.","topic":"Business uses"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Own-occupation","match":"Pays if you cannot do your own job"},{"term":"Any-occupation","match":"Pays only if you cannot do any suitable job"},{"term":"Probationary period","match":"One-time wait after issue, sickness only"},{"term":"Elimination period","match":"Wait after each disability begins"},{"term":"Flat partial benefit","match":"Fixed amount, usually 50% of full benefit"},{"term":"Residual benefit","match":"Percent of income lost times monthly benefit"}]},"ch16":{"title":"Chapter 16 - Private Insurance Plans for Seniors","flashcards":[{"front":"Medigap purpose and eligibility","back":"Private add-on that covers gaps (deductibles, coinsurance) in Medicare Parts A and B. Anyone enrolled in Medicare is eligible; no minimum age.","topic":"Medigap","memory":""},{"front":"Medigap plan letters","back":"Standardized since June 2010: A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N (ten plans).","topic":"Medigap","memory":""},{"front":"Medigap open enrollment","back":"6 months, starting when the person first enrolls in Part B at 65 or older. Guaranteed issue: no refusal and no rate-up.","topic":"Medigap","memory":""},{"front":"Five Medigap minimum standards","back":"Cover Parts A and B; adjust with Medicare law; preexisting exclusion max 6 months; cover Part B 20% coinsurance; 30-day free look.","topic":"Medigap","memory":""},{"front":"Medigap core benefits (all plans)","back":"Part A coinsurance plus 365 extra hospital days, Part B coinsurance, first 3 pints of blood, and hospice coinsurance.","topic":"Medigap","memory":""},{"front":"Foreign travel emergency in Medigap","back":"Optional benefit in certain plans, not standard in every plan and not in Plan A. Check the plan's benefit chart.","topic":"Medigap","memory":""},{"front":"Plan F vs Plan G","back":"Both cover Part B excess charges. F is closed to new Medicare beneficiaries after 2020 (MACRA); G is available.","topic":"Medigap","memory":""},{"front":"Plan K vs Plan L","back":"K covers 50% of most cost sharing; L covers 75%. Both pay 100% after the annual out-of-pocket limit.","topic":"Medigap","memory":"K = 50, L = 75."},{"front":"Medicare Select","back":"Medigap plan that uses a network of hospitals and doctors for full benefits. Emergencies are covered outside the network. Lower premiums.","topic":"Medicare Select","memory":""},{"front":"Medicare Part C plan types","back":"HMO, PPO, PSO (run by providers), and PFFS (any approved provider accepting plan terms). Only one Advantage plan at a time.","topic":"Part C","memory":""},{"front":"Part D late enrollment penalty","back":"1% per month of delay if the person did not join during the initial enrollment period.","topic":"Part D","memory":""},{"front":"LTC benefit trigger","back":"Unable to perform at least 2 of 6 ADLs for at least 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment.","topic":"Long-term care","memory":"2 of 6, 90 days."},{"front":"The six ADLs","back":"Bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring, continence, eating.","topic":"Long-term care","memory":""},{"front":"Calendar days vs service days (LTC elimination)","back":"Calendar: every consecutive day counts, faster access. Service days: only days care is received count, slower access.","topic":"Long-term care","memory":""},{"front":"LTC partnership program","back":"State-run, federally supported. Gives dollar-for-dollar asset protection from the Medicaid spend-down, equal to benefits paid.","topic":"Long-term care","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of a Medicare supplement (Medigap) policy?","choices":["Replace Medicare Parts A and B","Cover prescription drugs only","Pay for long-term custodial care","Fill gaps such as deductibles and coinsurance in Parts A and B"],"answer":3,"explain":"Medigap adds to Original Medicare. Replacing Parts A and B describes Medicare Advantage (Part C).","topic":"Medigap"},{"q":"Which Part C plan type is run by health care providers, who take on the financial risk?","choices":["HMO","PSO","PPO","PFFS"],"answer":1,"explain":"A provider-sponsored organization (PSO) is run by providers rather than insurers. PFFS lets members see any approved provider that accepts the plan's terms.","topic":"Part C"},{"q":"Under LTC elimination periods, calendar days differ from service days because calendar days:","choices":["Count only days care is received","Count every consecutive day, so benefits start sooner","Exclude weekends","Apply only to home care"],"answer":1,"explain":"Calendar days count every day, giving faster access. Service days count only days when care is actually received.","topic":"Long-term care"},{"q":"Medicare Select differs from standard Medigap because it:","choices":["Allows any provider with no limits","Costs more","Requires network providers for full benefits and has lower premiums","Replaces Part B"],"answer":2,"explain":"Medicare Select works like managed care, with lower premiums in exchange for using network providers. Emergencies are covered outside the network.","topic":"Medicare Select"},{"q":"How long is the Medigap open enrollment (guaranteed issue) period?","choices":["30 days","90 days","1 year","6 months"],"answer":3,"explain":"It lasts 6 months from the start of Part B at 65 or older. The 30-day figure is the Medigap free-look period.","topic":"Medigap"},{"q":"What share of most cost sharing does Medigap Plan L cover?","choices":["50%","25%","75%","100%"],"answer":2,"explain":"Plan L covers 75% (insured pays 25%). Plan K covers 50%.","topic":"Medigap"},{"q":"What is the usual LTC benefit trigger?","choices":["Unable to do 1 of 6 ADLs for 30 days","Unable to do 2 of 6 ADLs for at least 90 days","Unable to do 3 of 6 ADLs for 180 days","Unable to work for 2 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"The trigger is at least 2 of 6 ADLs for at least 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment.","topic":"Long-term care"},{"q":"A Medigap agent's first-year commission cannot exceed what percent of the subsequent-year commission?","choices":["100%","150%","300%","200%"],"answer":3,"explain":"First-year commission may not exceed 200% of the renewal commission.","topic":"Medigap"},{"q":"Which is NOT a core benefit included in every Medigap plan?","choices":["Foreign travel emergency coverage","First 3 pints of blood","365 extra hospital days","Hospice coinsurance"],"answer":0,"explain":"Foreign travel emergency is optional in certain plans (not Plan A). The other three are core benefits in every plan.","topic":"Medigap"},{"q":"Which statement about Medicare Part C (Medicare Advantage) is NOT true?","choices":["It covers long-term care","A person can have only one Advantage plan at a time","The person must be enrolled in Parts A and B","Medicare pays a fixed amount to the private plan"],"answer":0,"explain":"Part C does not cover long-term care. Medicare does not cover LTC at all.","topic":"Part C"},{"q":"Which is NOT one of the five Medigap minimum standards?","choices":["30-day free look","Cover the Part B 20% coinsurance","Preexisting condition exclusion of up to 12 months","Benefits adjust with Medicare law changes"],"answer":2,"explain":"The preexisting condition exclusion may not exceed 6 months, not 12.","topic":"Medigap"},{"q":"Which statement about combining Medigap and Medicare Advantage is correct?","choices":["They can be combined for full coverage","Medigap is required with Part C","Medigap replaces Part C","They cannot be combined; Part C replaces Parts A and B"],"answer":3,"explain":"Medigap supplements Original Medicare. Part C replaces Parts A and B, so the two are not combined.","topic":"Part C"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Ellen turns 65 and enrolls in Medicare Part B. She applies for a Medigap policy within her first 6 months. She has a heart condition.","q":"Can the insurer refuse her or rate her up?","choices":["Yes, because of the heart condition","Yes, but only with a 12-month wait","No, guaranteed issue applies during open enrollment","Only if she is under 70"],"answer":2,"explain":"During the 6-month open enrollment the insurer cannot refuse coverage or rate her up.","topic":"Medigap"},{"situation":"Frank can no longer bathe or dress himself and needs hands-on help. His LTC policy requires inability to do 2 of 6 ADLs for 90 days, and the need lasts 90 days.","q":"Does he meet the benefit trigger?","choices":["No, he needs 3 ADLs","No, the period must be 180 days","Only if he has cognitive impairment","Yes, 2 ADLs for at least 90 days"],"answer":3,"explain":"Bathing and dressing are 2 ADLs, lasting 90 days, which meets the trigger.","topic":"Long-term care"},{"situation":"Maria wants an LTC policy whose elimination period is satisfied as soon as possible, even though she will receive care only three times a week.","q":"Should she choose calendar days or service days?","choices":["Service days","Calendar days","Neither; elimination periods are fixed","Either, they work the same"],"answer":1,"explain":"Calendar days count every consecutive day, so benefits start sooner. Service days count only care days and slow access.","topic":"Long-term care"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Medigap","match":"Private add-on to Original Medicare Parts A and B"},{"term":"Medicare Advantage (Part C)","match":"Private plan that replaces Parts A and B"},{"term":"Plan F","match":"Closed to new Medicare beneficiaries after 2020"},{"term":"PSO","match":"Part C plan run by health care providers"},{"term":"Service days","match":"Only days care is actually received count"},{"term":"Partnership program","match":"Dollar-for-dollar protection from Medicaid spend-down"}]},"ch17":{"title":"Chapter 17 - Health Policy Provisions","flashcards":[{"front":"How many mandatory and optional provisions in the NAIC model law?","back":"12 mandatory provisions (required in every individual health policy) and 11 optional provisions (insurer's choice).","topic":"NAIC model law","memory":""},{"front":"Grace periods by payment frequency","back":"NAIC model law: weekly 7 days, monthly 10 days, quarterly or longer 31 days. Louisiana uses 30 days (life: 30 days or one month; health: 30 days).","topic":"Grace period","memory":"Weekly = a WEEK (7). Monthly = 10. Else a month and a day (31 model; LA = 30)."},{"front":"Entire contract provision","back":"Contract = policy + application + endorsements + riders. Agents cannot change it; only an authorized executive officer can.","topic":"Mandatory provisions","memory":""},{"front":"Notice of claim deadline","back":"Within 20 days of the loss, or as soon as reasonably possible.","topic":"Claims","memory":""},{"front":"Claim forms deadline","back":"The insurer must provide claim forms within 15 days of receiving notice of claim.","topic":"Claims","memory":""},{"front":"Proof of loss deadline","back":"90 days from the date of loss; extended to 1 year if the claimant is legally incapacitated.","topic":"Claims","memory":""},{"front":"Legal actions provision","back":"Wait at least 60 days after proof of loss before suing; file suit within 3 years.","topic":"Mandatory provisions","memory":""},{"front":"Reinstatement coverage start","back":"Accidents covered immediately; sickness after 10 days. Auto-reinstated if the insurer does not respond within 45 days.","topic":"Reinstatement","memory":""},{"front":"Free look period","back":"Standard: 10 days. Senior policies (LTC and Medicare supplement): 30 days. Starts when the policyholder receives the policy.","topic":"Free look","memory":""},{"front":"Change of occupation (optional provision)","back":"More hazardous job: maximum benefit reduced. Less hazardous job: premium reduced.","topic":"Optional provisions","memory":"MORE hazard = LESS benefit."},{"front":"Cancellation provision notice periods","back":"Insurer cancellation: 45 days written notice. Cancellation for nonpayment: 10 days notice.","topic":"Optional provisions","memory":""},{"front":"Guaranteed renewable vs noncancellable","back":"Guaranteed renewable: insurer cannot cancel (except nonpayment), premium can rise by class. Noncancellable: premium locked too.","topic":"Renewability","memory":""},{"front":"Absolute vs collateral assignment","back":"Absolute: full, permanent transfer of rights. Collateral: partial, temporary transfer to secure a loan.","topic":"Assignment","memory":""},{"front":"Revocable vs irrevocable beneficiary","back":"Revocable: can be changed anytime without consent. Irrevocable: change requires the beneficiary's written consent.","topic":"Beneficiaries","memory":""},{"front":"ACA dependent coverage age","back":"Dependent coverage until age 26. It is a dependent coverage requirement, not a conversion privilege.","topic":"Dependent coverage","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which provision states that the contract consists of the policy, application, endorsements, and riders?","choices":["Legal actions","Entire contract","Proof of loss","Insuring clause"],"answer":1,"explain":"The entire contract provision limits the contract to those documents. Outside statements do not count.","topic":"Mandatory provisions"},{"q":"What does the change of occupation optional provision do if the insured moves to a more hazardous job?","choices":["Raises the premium","Cancels the policy","Increases the benefit","Reduces the maximum benefit"],"answer":3,"explain":"A more hazardous job reduces the benefit. A less hazardous job reduces the premium.","topic":"Optional provisions"},{"q":"Which provision says the insurer pays only a proportionate amount if disability benefits would exceed the insured's earnings?","choices":["Relation of earnings","Misstatement of age","Unpaid premiums","Conformity with state statutes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Relation of earnings (average earnings clause) prevents over-insurance.","topic":"Optional provisions"},{"q":"Under the right of assignment provision, benefits can be:","choices":["Paid only to the insured","Paid only after the insured is reimbursed","Paid directly to the health provider","Paid only to the estate"],"answer":2,"explain":"Assignment lets benefits go directly to the provider so the insured does not pay first and wait for reimbursement.","topic":"Other provisions"},{"q":"Under the NAIC model law, what is the grace period for a monthly premium?","choices":["7 days","10 days","31 days","45 days"],"answer":1,"explain":"Monthly is 10 days. 7 days is weekly, and 31 days is the model-law figure for quarterly or longer (Louisiana uses 30 days).","topic":"Grace period"},{"q":"How many days after receiving notice must the insurer provide claim forms?","choices":["10","20","30","15"],"answer":3,"explain":"The insurer has 15 days. Notice of claim itself is due within 20 days of the loss.","topic":"Claims"},{"q":"How long must a claimant wait after proof of loss before filing suit?","choices":["At least 60 days","At least 3 years","At least 90 days","No waiting period"],"answer":0,"explain":"The wait is at least 60 days; suit must be filed within 3 years. 90 days is the proof of loss deadline.","topic":"Mandatory provisions"},{"q":"How many days of written notice must an insurer give to cancel under the cancellation provision (not for nonpayment)?","choices":["10","30","45","90"],"answer":2,"explain":"Insurer cancellation requires 45 days notice. Cancellation for nonpayment requires 10 days.","topic":"Optional provisions"},{"q":"Which is NOT one of the 12 mandatory provisions?","choices":["Grace period","Change of beneficiary","Physical exam and autopsy","Relation of earnings"],"answer":3,"explain":"Relation of earnings is an optional provision. The other three are mandatory.","topic":"Mandatory provisions"},{"q":"Which statement about reinstatement is NOT correct?","choices":["Accident coverage resumes immediately","Sickness coverage resumes immediately","The insurer's silence for 45 days results in reinstatement","Back premiums must be paid"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sickness coverage resumes only after 10 days, to prevent adverse selection.","topic":"Reinstatement"},{"q":"Which statement about a guaranteed renewable policy is NOT true?","choices":["The insurer cannot cancel except for nonpayment","Premiums may rise by class","Premiums are locked for the life of the policy","Premiums cannot be raised for one individual alone"],"answer":2,"explain":"Locked premiums describe a noncancellable policy. Guaranteed renewable premiums can rise, but by class, not for one individual.","topic":"Renewability"},{"q":"Which statement about the free-look period is NOT correct?","choices":["Standard is 10 days","Senior policies get 30 days","It starts when the policy is received","If canceled, the insurer still pays claims during the period"],"answer":3,"explain":"If the policy is canceled during the free look, the insurer has no liability for claims.","topic":"Free look"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Ben's quarterly premium is due March 1 and he does not pay. He dies on March 20.","q":"What happens to the death benefit?","choices":["The policy lapsed, so nothing is paid","The full face amount is paid","The benefit is paid minus the unpaid premium","Only premiums paid are refunded"],"answer":2,"explain":"The quarterly grace period is about a month (31 days under the model law; 30 in Louisiana), so March 20 is inside it. The unpaid premium is deducted.","topic":"Grace period"},{"situation":"Rita's waiver of premium rider ends at age 60. She becomes totally and permanently disabled at age 61.","q":"Does the waiver apply?","choices":["Yes, always","Yes, after a 90-day wait","Only if premiums are repaid","No, the rider had already ended"],"answer":3,"explain":"The disability began after the rider's cutoff age, so the waiver does not apply.","topic":"Riders"},{"situation":"The insurer never responds to Carl's reinstatement application.","q":"What happens?","choices":["Automatic reinstatement after 45 days, with sickness coverage 10 days later","The application is denied after 45 days","Reinstatement after 90 days, all coverage immediate","Carl must reapply as a new applicant"],"answer":0,"explain":"No response within 45 days means automatic reinstatement. Sickness coverage starts after 10 days; accidents are covered at once.","topic":"Reinstatement"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Notice of claim","match":"Due within 20 days of the loss"},{"term":"Proof of loss","match":"Due within 90 days of the date of loss"},{"term":"Absolute assignment","match":"Full and permanent transfer of rights"},{"term":"Collateral assignment","match":"Partial, temporary transfer securing a loan"},{"term":"Irrevocable beneficiary","match":"Change requires written consent"},{"term":"Noncancellable","match":"Cannot cancel; premiums locked"}]},"ch18":{"title":"Chapter 18 - Underwriting and Policy Issue (Health)","flashcards":[{"front":"Primary goal of underwriting","back":"Evaluate and select risks to protect against adverse selection (more high-risk people seeking coverage).","topic":"Underwriting","memory":""},{"front":"Three parts of the application","back":"Part I general, Part II medical (must be completed for every application), Part III agent's report.","topic":"Application","memory":""},{"front":"Applicant vs proposed insured","back":"Applicant completes the application. Proposed insured is the person whose life or health is being considered; they may differ.","topic":"Application","memory":""},{"front":"Representation vs warranty","back":"Representation: true to the best of knowledge, matters only if material. Warranty: must be completely true; breach can void the policy.","topic":"Representations","memory":"Representation = best of my knowledge. Warranty = guarantee every word."},{"front":"Misrepresentation vs fraud","back":"Misrepresentation is an untrue statement; it matters if material. Fraud is a material misrepresentation made deliberately.","topic":"Representations","memory":""},{"front":"Medical Information Bureau (MIB)","back":"Member insurers only. Used only for underwriting and claims. Uses code numbers for conditions and helps uncover omissions and fraud.","topic":"MIB","memory":""},{"front":"Attending physician statement (APS)","back":"Requested when more detail on a medical condition is needed. Requires the applicant's signed authorization.","topic":"Underwriting sources","memory":""},{"front":"Risk classes and lowest premium","back":"Preferred (lowest premium), standard, substandard (rated up), uninsurable (declined). Special class gets an exclusion or modified policy.","topic":"Risk classes","memory":""},{"front":"Insurable interest in health insurance","back":"Requires a FINANCIAL interest (loss from medical expenses or disability). Sentimental attachment alone is not enough.","topic":"Insurable interest","memory":""},{"front":"Conditional receipt","back":"Most common. Coverage starts if the applicant is insurable, effective the later of application date or exam date.","topic":"Premium receipts","memory":"CONDITIONAL = if insurable."},{"front":"Binding receipt","back":"Coverage guaranteed until the application is denied, even if later found uninsurable. High risk for the insurer.","topic":"Premium receipts","memory":"BINDING = until denied."},{"front":"Inspection receipt","back":"Temporary delivery of the policy without payment, so the applicant can review before buying. Less used because of free look.","topic":"Premium receipts","memory":"INSPECTION = look first."},{"front":"Constructive delivery","back":"Delivery without physical transfer; the insurer gives up all control to someone acting for the owner. Not if the agent must hold the policy.","topic":"Policy delivery","memory":""},{"front":"Backdating limit","back":"Effective date set before the application date to lower age and premium: up to 6 months. Not allowed for variable contracts.","topic":"Key dates","memory":""},{"front":"Earned vs unearned premium","back":"Earned: portion the insurer has earned (income). Unearned: portion not yet earned, a liability refunded if the policy is canceled.","topic":"Premiums","memory":""}],"questions":[{"q":"Which application part must be completed for EVERY application?","choices":["Part I (general)","Part II (medical)","Part III (agent's report)","None are required"],"answer":1,"explain":"Part II (health history) is required for every application. Part III is the agent's observations.","topic":"Application"},{"q":"A statement that must be completely true, with breach able to void the policy even if unintentional, is a:","choices":["Representation","Counter-offer","Warranty","Misrepresentation"],"answer":2,"explain":"That is a warranty. A representation need only be true to the best of knowledge and material.","topic":"Representations"},{"q":"Which receipt guarantees coverage until the application is denied?","choices":["Binding","Conditional","Inspection","Delivery"],"answer":0,"explain":"Binding receipts cover the applicant until denial. A conditional receipt requires the applicant to be insurable.","topic":"Premium receipts"},{"q":"Which document is SPECIFIC to the product being sold and includes agent, company, premiums, and cost indexes?","choices":["Buyer's guide","Policy summary","Outline of coverage","Consumer report"],"answer":1,"explain":"The policy summary is product-specific. The buyer's guide is general information about insurance.","topic":"Disclosures"},{"q":"What is the maximum backdating period?","choices":["30 days","3 months","1 year","6 months"],"answer":3,"explain":"Backdating is limited to 6 months and is not allowed for variable contracts.","topic":"Key dates"},{"q":"What is the standard free-look period for an individual policy under this chapter?","choices":["10 days","20 days","31 days","60 days"],"answer":0,"explain":"Standard is at least 10 days; direct response and mail-order insurers give at least 30 days. It starts at delivery.","topic":"Policy delivery"},{"q":"When the policy is replaced, who must sign the notice of replacement?","choices":["Only the applicant","Only the agent","The insurer's underwriter","Both the agent and the applicant"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both the agent and the applicant sign the notice of replacement.","topic":"Replacement"},{"q":"Rates are calculated assuming which premium mode, so more frequent payment costs more in total?","choices":["Monthly","Quarterly","Annual","Weekly"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rates assume annual payment. Weekly and monthly modes cost more in total.","topic":"Premiums"},{"q":"If an agent changes an application, which is NOT allowed?","choices":["Correcting errors with the applicant initialing","Changes with the applicant's written consent","Changes without the applicant's written consent","Returning an incomplete application to the agent"],"answer":2,"explain":"No changes may be made without the applicant's written consent; corrections must be initialed.","topic":"Application"},{"q":"Which is NOT true of the MIB?","choices":["Only member companies can access it","It can be used for underwriting and claims","It can be used for any marketing purpose","It uses code numbers for conditions"],"answer":2,"explain":"MIB information can be used ONLY for underwriting and claims processing, not marketing.","topic":"MIB"},{"q":"Which statement about a trial application is NOT correct?","choices":["It is submitted without the first premium","It is a formal offer that creates coverage","The applicant is inviting an offer","No premium receipt is issued"],"answer":1,"explain":"A trial application is not a formal offer; the applicant invites the insurer to make one.","topic":"Applications"},{"q":"Which is NOT true about insurable interest in health insurance?","choices":["It involves a financial loss","It is required for a policy to be issued","Emotional attachment alone is enough","It relates to medical expenses or disability"],"answer":2,"explain":"Insurable interest requires a financial interest; sentimental attachment alone does not establish it.","topic":"Insurable interest"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Nina's application says she was never treated for asthma. She was treated two years ago and forgot. The insurer learns this after a claim.","q":"How is her statement treated in most states?","choices":["A warranty that automatically voids the policy","A representation, relevant only if material","Fraud, because it was untrue","Irrelevant to the contract"],"answer":1,"explain":"Most states treat application statements as representations. It matters only if material, and an honest mistake is not fraud.","topic":"Representations"},{"situation":"An agent gives Owen his policy but tells him to hold it until he confirms he is still in good health.","q":"Has constructive delivery occurred?","choices":["Yes, the agent has the policy","Yes, because the premium was paid","Probably not, because control was not given up","Yes, after 10 days"],"answer":2,"explain":"Constructive delivery requires the insurer to give up all control. A hold-until-healthy instruction defeats that.","topic":"Policy delivery"},{"situation":"A 42-year-old with a history of heart bypass surgery applies. The insurer offers coverage at a higher premium.","q":"What type of offer is this?","choices":["A preferred offer","A binding receipt","A trial application","A substandard counter-offer"],"answer":3,"explain":"A counter-offer declines the standard rate and offers a rated policy at a higher premium for an impaired risk.","topic":"Counter-offers"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Representation","match":"True to best of knowledge; matters if material"},{"term":"Warranty","match":"Must be completely true; breach can void policy"},{"term":"Conditional receipt","match":"Coverage if the applicant is insurable"},{"term":"Buyer's guide","match":"General resource on insurance concepts"},{"term":"Experience rating","match":"Group's own claims history sets its price"},{"term":"Community rating","match":"Everyone in an area pays the same price"}]},"ch19":{"title":"Bonus - Social Insurance Study Tool Slides","flashcards":[{"front":"Who is eligible for Medicare?","back":"People 65 or older, people under 65 with qualifying disabilities, and people of any age with ESRD or ALS.","topic":"Medicare","memory":""},{"front":"Medicare Part A: coverage and cost","back":"Inpatient hospital, SNF, hospice, some home health. Usually free if you or your spouse paid Medicare taxes 10+ years.","topic":"Medicare Part A","memory":""},{"front":"Part A coinsurance trigger","back":"Coinsurance applies after 60 days in a hospital or 20 days in a skilled nursing facility.","topic":"Medicare Part A","memory":""},{"front":"Medicare Part B basics","back":"Optional medical coverage: doctor, outpatient, preventive, equipment. Monthly premium (income-adjusted), annual deductible, then 20% coinsurance.","topic":"Medicare Part B","memory":""},{"front":"Part B late enrollment penalty","back":"10% increase for each 12-month delay in enrolling.","topic":"Medicare Part B","memory":""},{"front":"Medicare Part C (Advantage)","back":"Offered by private insurers as an alternative to Original Medicare. Must be enrolled in A and B. Often includes Part D and extras.","topic":"Medicare Part C","memory":"All-in-one."},{"front":"What does Part C not cover?","back":"It must cover A and B except hospice; Original Medicare still pays hospice.","topic":"Medicare Part C","memory":""},{"front":"Medicare Part D","back":"Prescription drugs through private plans. Must be enrolled in A or B. Annual out-of-pocket cap; the donut hole was eliminated January 1, 2025.","topic":"Medicare Part D","memory":""},{"front":"Medicare enrollment window","back":"7 months: 3 before the 65th birthday month, the birthday month, and 3 after.","topic":"Medicare","memory":""},{"front":"Medicaid in one line","back":"Joint federal-state program for low-income people (children, pregnant women, elderly, disabled). States administer; rules vary by state.","topic":"Medicaid","memory":"Medicaid = Aid for the poor."},{"front":"Medicaid mandatory vs optional benefits","back":"Mandatory: hospital, physician, lab/X-ray, nursing home, home health, family planning, pediatric, maternity. Optional: prescriptions, dental, vision, PT.","topic":"Medicaid","memory":""},{"front":"Medicaid and long-term care","back":"Medicaid is the primary payer of long-term nursing home care and covers custodial care; Medicare typically does not.","topic":"Medicaid","memory":""},{"front":"Social Security retirement eligibility","back":"40 work credits (about 10 years). Earliest age 62 (reduced); benefit increases for delay up to age 70.","topic":"Social Security","memory":""},{"front":"SSDI waiting period and Medicare","back":"5-month waiting period (waived for ALS). Medicare begins 24 months after benefits start, except ALS.","topic":"Social Security","memory":""},{"front":"SSDI vs SSI","back":"SSDI is based on work history, funded by FICA. SSI is needs-based, no work history, funded by general tax revenues.","topic":"Social Security","memory":"SSDI = work history. SSI = need."}],"questions":[{"q":"Which Medicare part covers prescription drugs through private plans?","choices":["Part A","Part B","Part C only","Part D"],"answer":3,"explain":"Part D is the prescription drug part. Part C can include drug coverage but is the all-in-one Advantage option.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"Which Medicare part is usually free if you or your spouse paid Medicare taxes for 10 or more years?","choices":["Part A","Part B","Part C","Part D"],"answer":0,"explain":"Part A (hospital) is usually premium-free. Part B carries a monthly premium.","topic":"Medicare Part A"},{"q":"What does Part B cover?","choices":["Inpatient hospital stays only","Doctor services, outpatient care, preventive services, and equipment","Private insurer all-in-one plans","Long-term nursing home care"],"answer":1,"explain":"Part B is medical insurance. Inpatient hospital is Part A, and long-term custodial nursing home care is paid by Medicaid.","topic":"Medicare Part B"},{"q":"Which program is the primary payer of long-term nursing home care?","choices":["Medicare Part A","Social Security","Medicaid","Part D"],"answer":2,"explain":"Medicaid pays for long-term nursing home and custodial care. Medicare does not cover custodial care.","topic":"Medicaid"},{"q":"What coinsurance applies under Part B after the annual deductible?","choices":["10%","20%","50%","None"],"answer":1,"explain":"Part B pays after the deductible with 20% coinsurance owed by the beneficiary.","topic":"Medicare Part B"},{"q":"How many work credits are needed for retirement benefits and to be fully insured?","choices":["4","20","40","35"],"answer":2,"explain":"40 credits (about 10 years; most earn 4 per year). 35 is the number of highest-earning years used for AIME.","topic":"Social Security"},{"q":"How long after SSDI benefits start does Medicare eligibility begin (except ALS)?","choices":["5 months","12 months","36 months","24 months"],"answer":3,"explain":"Medicare starts 24 months after disability benefits begin. The 5-month figure is the SSDI waiting period.","topic":"Social Security"},{"q":"The Medicare enrollment window around the 65th birthday is:","choices":["7 months","3 months","6 months","12 months"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is 7 months: 3 before the birthday month, the month itself, and 3 after.","topic":"Medicare"},{"q":"Which is NOT required of a Part C (Medicare Advantage) plan?","choices":["Cover everything in Parts A and B","Cover hospice care","Be offered by a private insurer","Require enrollment in Parts A and B"],"answer":1,"explain":"Part C covers everything in A and B except hospice, which Original Medicare still pays.","topic":"Medicare Part C"},{"q":"Which statement about Medicaid is NOT true?","choices":["States run it under federal rules","Enrollment is year-round","It is mainly for people with low income","It has one nationwide set of eligibility rules"],"answer":3,"explain":"Medicaid eligibility and benefits vary by state within minimum federal guidelines.","topic":"Medicaid"},{"q":"Which statement about SSI is NOT true?","choices":["It is needs-based","It requires a work history","It is funded by general tax revenues","It is for the elderly, blind, or disabled with limited income"],"answer":1,"explain":"SSI does not depend on work history. SSDI is the work-history benefit.","topic":"Social Security"},{"q":"Which statement about Part D is NOT true?","choices":["There is an annual out-of-pocket cap","A late penalty can apply without creditable coverage","The donut hole still exists","Enrollment requires Part A or B"],"answer":2,"explain":"The coverage gap (donut hole) was eliminated on January 1, 2025.","topic":"Medicare Part D"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Ray is turning 65 and has not signed up for Medicare yet. His birthday is in June.","q":"When is his enrollment window?","choices":["Only the month of his birthday","The 3 months after his birthday","March through September","The 12 months after his birthday"],"answer":2,"explain":"The 7-month window is 3 months before the birthday month (March), June, and 3 months after (September).","topic":"Medicare"},{"situation":"Dana is a low-income senior who has Medicare and also qualifies for Medicaid. She is also in a nursing home for long-term custodial care.","q":"What does her coverage look like?","choices":["She is a dual eligible; Medicaid may help pay Medicare costs and covers long-term nursing home care","Medicare alone pays for custodial care","She must drop Medicare","She is only eligible for SSI"],"answer":0,"explain":"People with both programs are dual eligibles. Medicaid may pay Medicare premiums and copays and covers long-term custodial care.","topic":"Medicaid"},{"situation":"A worker becomes disabled and starts SSDI. Another person has never worked and has very low income and resources, and is disabled.","q":"Which benefit does each person generally receive?","choices":["Both receive SSDI","Worker gets SSI; non-worker gets SSDI","Worker gets SSDI; non-worker may qualify for SSI","Neither qualifies"],"answer":2,"explain":"SSDI is based on work history. SSI is based on need and does not require work history.","topic":"Social Security"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Medicare Part A","match":"Hospital insurance, usually premium-free"},{"term":"Medicare Part B","match":"Optional medical coverage with 20% coinsurance"},{"term":"Medicare Part C","match":"Private all-in-one Medicare Advantage plan"},{"term":"Medicare Part D","match":"Prescription drug coverage through private plans"},{"term":"Medicaid","match":"Joint federal-state aid for low-income people"},{"term":"SSI","match":"Needs-based benefit, no work history required"}]},"addendum":{"title":"Addendum: Louisiana Regulation and Exam Blueprint","flashcards":[{"front":"Series 103 exam: how many questions and how much time?","back":"150 questions in 160 minutes. Combined life, health, and accident exam. Passing score is 70%.","topic":"Exam format","memory":"103 = 150 q, 160 min"},{"front":"Series 101 and 102 exams: questions and time?","back":"Each is 100 questions in 120 minutes. 101 is life; 102 is health and accident. Passing score is 70%.","topic":"Exam format","memory":""},{"front":"Louisiana passing score on the PSI exam?","back":"70% (R.S. 22:1545(A)).","topic":"Exam format","memory":""},{"front":"Series 103 weight: Louisiana insurance regulation?","back":"23%, the largest single domain on the combined exam.","topic":"Series 103 weights","memory":"23 = biggest"},{"front":"Series 103 weight: Licensing and regulation?","back":"Only 1%. Do not confuse it with Louisiana insurance regulation (23%).","topic":"Series 103 weights","memory":""},{"front":"Series 103 weights: annuities, and life/health/accident basics?","back":"Annuities 7%. Life, health, and accident insurance basics 14%.","topic":"Series 103 weights","memory":"Basics are double annuities"},{"front":"Series 103 weights: policy provisions/riders and types of life policies?","back":"Life policy provisions, options, and riders 13%. Types of life insurance policies 8%.","topic":"Series 103 weights","memory":""},{"front":"Louisiana life free look period?","back":"10 days from receipt of the policy (R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a)).","topic":"Life policy rules","memory":""},{"front":"Louisiana incontestability: life versus health?","back":"Life: 2 years (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)). Health: 3 years (R.S. 22:975).","topic":"Policy rules","memory":"Life 2, health 3"},{"front":"Louisiana grace period: life versus health?","back":"Life: 30 days or one month, at the insurer's option (R.S. 22:931(A)(1)). Health: 30 days (R.S. 22:977).","topic":"Policy rules","memory":""},{"front":"Louisiana life reinstatement window?","back":"Within 3 years of default (R.S. 22:931(A)(9)).","topic":"Life policy rules","memory":""},{"front":"Youngest age a minor may contract for life, health, or accident insurance in Louisiana?","back":"15 (R.S. 22:852(B)). Age of majority is 18 (Civil Code art. 29).","topic":"Contract capacity","memory":""},{"front":"Louisiana CE requirement per 2-year renewal?","back":"24 hours, including at least 3 hours of ethics (R.S. 22:1573(C)).","topic":"Licensing and CE","memory":"24 total, 3 ethics"},{"front":"Louisiana annuity training requirement?","back":"One-time 4 hours, separate from regular CE.","topic":"Licensing and CE","memory":""},{"front":"Louisiana guaranty association limits per person?","back":"Death $300,000; cash surrender and withdrawal values $100,000; health $500,000; annuity present value $250,000; $500,000 aggregate (R.S. 22:2083(C)(2)).","topic":"Guaranty association","memory":"300 / 100 / 500 / 250 / 500 cap"}],"questions":[{"q":"On the Series 103 exam, what is the weight of Louisiana insurance regulation?","choices":["10%","14%","23%","1%"],"answer":2,"explain":"Louisiana insurance regulation is 23% of Series 103. The 10% figure belongs to Series 101 and 102.","topic":"Series 103 weights"},{"q":"On the Series 103 exam, what is the weight of the Licensing and regulation domain?","choices":["1%","7%","14%","23%"],"answer":0,"explain":"Licensing and regulation is just 1% on Series 103. The 23% belongs to Louisiana insurance regulation.","topic":"Series 103 weights"},{"q":"Which domain has the highest weight on the Series 103 exam?","choices":["Life, health, and accident insurance basics","Life policy provisions, options, and riders","Louisiana insurance regulation","Annuities"],"answer":2,"explain":"Louisiana regulation is 23%. The next largest is basics at 14%.","topic":"Series 103 weights"},{"q":"Which Series 103 domain weight is NOT correct?","choices":["Annuities 7%","Disability income and related insurance 4%","Federal tax considerations 2%","Group health insurance 13%"],"answer":3,"explain":"Group health insurance is 3% on Series 103. The 13% figure is life policy provisions, options, and riders.","topic":"Series 103 weights"},{"q":"On the Series 101 (life) exam, which domain carries the highest weight?","choices":["Types of life insurance policies","Life policy provisions, options, and riders","Louisiana insurance regulation","Annuities"],"answer":1,"explain":"provisions, options, and riders is 26% on Series 101. Louisiana regulation is only 10% there.","topic":"Series 101/102 weights"},{"q":"How long is the Louisiana life insurance free look period?","choices":["20 days from application","31 days from issue","30 days from receipt","10 days from receipt"],"answer":3,"explain":"R.S. 22:931(A)(10)(a): 10 days from receipt of the policy.","topic":"Life policy rules"},{"q":"What is the Louisiana incontestability period for a health policy?","choices":["1 year","2 years","3 years","5 years"],"answer":2,"explain":"R.S. 22:975: 3 years for health. Life is 2 years (R.S. 22:931(A)(2)).","topic":"Policy rules"},{"q":"Within what time after default may a lapsed Louisiana life policy be reinstated under the statutory provision?","choices":["1 year","3 years","5 years","7 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"R.S. 22:931(A)(9): within 3 years of default.","topic":"Life policy rules"},{"q":"What is the aggregate Louisiana guaranty association limit per individual?","choices":["$300,000","$250,000","$100,000","$500,000"],"answer":3,"explain":"R.S. 22:2083(C)(2): $500,000 total per person. $300,000 is only the death benefit cap.","topic":"Guaranty association"},{"q":"At what minimum age may a Louisiana minor contract for life, health, or accident insurance?","choices":["18","16","12","15"],"answer":3,"explain":"R.S. 22:852(B): a minor 15 or older may contract. The age of majority is 18 (Civil Code art. 29).","topic":"Contract capacity"},{"q":"Which statement about Louisiana policy rules is NOT correct?","choices":["Life grace period is 30 days or one month","Health grace period is 30 days","Life grace period is 31 days","Life free look is 10 days from receipt"],"answer":2,"explain":"R.S. 22:931(A)(1) sets 30 days or one month. Louisiana does not use a 31-day life grace period.","topic":"Policy rules"},{"q":"Which is NOT a correct Louisiana guaranty association limit?","choices":["Death benefits $300,000","Life cash surrender and withdrawal values $100,000","Health benefits $500,000","Annuity present value $500,000"],"answer":3,"explain":"R.S. 22:2083(C)(2): annuity present value is $250,000. The $500,000 figure is the aggregate cap per person.","topic":"Guaranty association"}],"scenarios":[{"situation":"Dana wants to take life, health, and accident licensing in a single sitting rather than two separate exams. She is checking the length and passing mark of the PSI exam her registration should list.","q":"Which exam format applies?","choices":["Series 103: 100 questions, 120 minutes, 70%","Series 101: 150 questions, 160 minutes, 70%","Series 103: 150 questions, 160 minutes, 70%","Series 102: 100 questions, 120 minutes, 80%"],"answer":2,"explain":"Series 103 is the combined exam: 150 questions in 160 minutes, passing score 70%. Series 101 and 102 are 100 questions in 120 minutes each.","topic":"Exam format"},{"situation":"A Louisiana insurer issued an individual life policy. The insured dies 18 months later, and the insurer finds a misstatement on the application and considers contesting the claim.","q":"Under the Louisiana life incontestability provision, is the policy still contestable?","choices":["No, the period is 1 year","Yes, the 2-year period has not ended","No, the period is 3 years and has ended","Yes, but only in the first 10 days"],"answer":1,"explain":"R.S. 22:931(A)(2) sets a 2-year incontestability period for life, and 18 months is inside it. The 3-year period applies to health (R.S. 22:975).","topic":"Policy rules"},{"situation":"Marcus is renewing his Louisiana producer license. He completed 24 CE hours this cycle, but only 2 were ethics. He also asks if his annuity training counts toward CE.","q":"What is the correct conclusion?","choices":["He is short on ethics: at least 3 ethics hours are required, and annuity training is separate","He meets CE; 24 hours is all that matters","He needs 3 ethics hours, but annuity training counts toward CE","He needs 30 hours total"],"answer":0,"explain":"R.S. 22:1573(C): 24 hours with at least 3 ethics per 2-year cycle. The one-time 4-hour annuity training is separate from CE.","topic":"Licensing and CE"}],"pairs":[{"term":"Life free look","match":"10 days from receipt"},{"term":"Life incontestability","match":"2 years"},{"term":"Health incontestability","match":"3 years"},{"term":"Minimum minor contract age","match":"15 years old"},{"term":"Series 103 exam","match":"150 questions, 160 minutes"},{"term":"Annuity training","match":"One-time 4 hours, separate from CE"}]}};
